window.SITE_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Choose The Jobs You Are Fitting","section":"Build your delivery checklist","url":"delivery-prep-builder.html#delivery-builder-heading","summary":"Work through driver fit first, then the parts groups. Dependencies are added automatically where one choice requires another. 1. Driver fit 2. Body & cockpit 3. Brakes, fuel & data 4. Setup & service 5. Review Step 1 of…","keywords":"Work through driver fit first, then the parts groups. Dependencies are added automatically where one choice requires another. 1. Driver fit 2. Body & cockpit 3. Brakes, fuel & data 4. Setup & service 5. Review Step 1 of 5"},{"title":"Driver Fit","section":"Build your delivery checklist","url":"delivery-prep-builder.html#builder-fit-heading","summary":"Start with helmet clearance. The generated checklist will only show the path that matches your measurement. Helmet clearance Measure with the driver belted in, helmet and FHR on, in the normal driving position. Not…","keywords":"Start with helmet clearance. The generated checklist will only show the path that matches your measurement. Helmet clearance Measure with the driver belted in, helmet and FHR on, in the normal driving position. Not checked yet Keep a measurement job in the generated checklist. 5 cm or more The car clears the minimum measurement in the current seat position. Less than 5 cm Tilt the seat back with front spacers, then measure again. Seat plan With 5 cm or more clearance, choose whether to keep the original Tillett seat or fit a bead seat by preference. Keep the original Tillett seat Use the supplied seat if fit, reach, belts, and clearance are all acceptable. Fit a bead seat Use a bead seat for fit, support, or preference even though clearance is compliant. Spacer adjustment Fit spacers under the front of the seat to tilt it back and lower the driver's helmet, then recheck clearance. Not rechecked yet Keep the spacer and recheck jobs in the generated checklist. 5 cm or more after spacers Clearance is now compliant, so you can choose the seat plan. Still less than 5 cm after spacers A bead seat is required if spacers do not create the minimum clearance. Seat plan after spacers With clearance restored to 5 cm or more, choose whether to keep the Tillett seat or move to a bead seat. Keep the original Tillett seat Use the tilted seat position if driver fit and belt geometry remain correct. Fit a bead seat Use a bead seat for fit, support, or preference after the spacer adjustment. Helmet clearance has not been checked yet. Step 2 of 5"},{"title":"Body & Cockpit","section":"Build your delivery checklist","url":"delivery-prep-builder.html#builder-body-heading","summary":"Choose the body, cockpit, and visibility jobs you are fitting after handover. Passenger side impact bar Adds side impact protection; includes anti-slip tape for the impact bars. Half sidescreens Common race-prep upgrade…","keywords":"Choose the body, cockpit, and visibility jobs you are fitting after handover. Passenger side impact bar Adds side impact protection; includes anti-slip tape for the impact bars. Half sidescreens Common race-prep upgrade from full sidescreens; includes the required race mirror package. Caterham race mirror package Required with half sidescreens; left, right, and centre visibility must be effective. Pitking centre mirror Optional centre mirror choice to improve rear visibility. Optional steering wheel Choose the Momo suede wheel or another compliant option based on driver fit. Step 3 of 5"},{"title":"Brakes, Fuel & Data","section":"Build your delivery checklist","url":"delivery-prep-builder.html#builder-brakes-heading","summary":"Pick the common brake, fuel, timing, and camera jobs. Competition brake pads Front and rear race pad fitment before shakedown or testing. Race master cylinder cap Permitted race cap option; removes the low-fluid…","keywords":"Pick the common brake, fuel, timing, and camera jobs. Competition brake pads Front and rear race pad fitment before shakedown or testing. Race master cylinder cap Permitted race cap option; removes the low-fluid warning. Mocal fuel drain hose Fuel draining aid; confirm the route against permitted fuel-drain rules. Dash timer Simple Salter-style timer for stint, race, or session timing. Additional foot camera mount Adds a dedicated pedal/footwork camera angle. Camera lens protection Protects exposed camera lenses from stones, debris, and paddock handling. Step 4 of 5"},{"title":"Setup & Service","section":"Build your delivery checklist","url":"delivery-prep-builder.html#builder-setup-heading","summary":"Choose the setup parts you want included in the generated work list. Front anti-roll bar Permitted front ARB option for tuning balance before setup work. Impact bar anti-slip tape Applied to impact bars because you…","keywords":"Choose the setup parts you want included in the generated work list. Front anti-roll bar Permitted front ARB option for tuning balance before setup work. Impact bar anti-slip tape Applied to impact bars because you stand on the bar when climbing over the cage. Step 5 of 5"},{"title":"Review & Use Checklist","section":"Build your delivery checklist","url":"delivery-prep-builder.html#builder-review-heading","summary":"The delivery checklist will use this saved selection until you edit it again. Helmet clearance has not been checked yet. Showing the recommended prep list. Use recommended Clear choices Save and view checklist Previous…","keywords":"The delivery checklist will use this saved selection until you edit it again. Helmet clearance has not been checked yet. Showing the recommended prep list. Use recommended Clear choices Save and view checklist Previous Next"},{"title":"Delivery Prep To-Do List","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#delivery-prep-to-do-list","summary":"This is the practical job list to work through after handover and before the first event. Some items are directly reflected in the permitted-parts reference; others are reliability or workshop checks, so confirm…","keywords":"This is the practical job list to work through after handover and before the first event. Some items are directly reflected in the permitted-parts reference; others are reliability or workshop checks, so confirm anything not explicitly listed before treating it as a regulation-backed modification. Selected prep jobs"},{"title":"Your Custom Prep List","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#delivery-builder-heading","summary":"The checklist below uses the prep jobs saved in the builder. Driver-fit and safety checks stay included automatically. Showing the recommended prep list. Helmet clearance has not been checked yet. Edit prep jobs View…","keywords":"The checklist below uses the prep jobs saved in the builder. Driver-fit and safety checks stay included automatically. Showing the recommended prep list. Helmet clearance has not been checked yet. Edit prep jobs View checklist Generated workshop list"},{"title":"Your Delivery Prep Checklist","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#your-delivery-prep-checklist","summary":"Showing the recommended prep list.","keywords":"Showing the recommended prep list."},{"title":"Driver Fit & Safety","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#driver-fit-safety","summary":"Do this first. Seat position, belt geometry, visibility, and helmet clearance affect every other setup decision. Complete a full driver fit session Set pedals, seat position, harnesses, head restraint, steering reach,…","keywords":"Do this first. Seat position, belt geometry, visibility, and helmet clearance affect every other setup decision. Complete a full driver fit session Set pedals, seat position, harnesses, head restraint, steering reach, mirrors, and helmet clearance together so the car is comfortable before testing. Check harness and FHR shoulder belt routing Recheck after any seat change. Shoulder straps must not run upwards, FHR use should be no more than 20 degrees below horizontal, HANS/FHR users should use the inner upper harness positions, crotch straps should be independently mounted, and shoulder strap bolts should protrude below the chassis rail. Set head restraint position Adjust it as close to the helmet/FHR as possible in the normal belted-in driving position. Check foam filler between seat back and bulkhead Mandatory per the regulations: fill any space between the driver's seat back and the seat-back bulkhead. Fit the supplied arm restraints They come with the car but still need fitting and adjustment. Set them so your hands cannot pass the roll cage, but all controls and the centre mirror remain reachable. Wear them on the wrist and mark the shorter right-hand strap. Trim and tape roll cage padding The supplied FIA padding comes fitted with tie wraps. Remove the tie wraps, shape the foam, trim the foam ends so it is easier to tape neatly into position, then secure it anywhere the helmet could contact the cage."},{"title":"Parts To Fit","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#parts-to-fit","summary":"This section is generated from your saved builder choices. Edit prep jobs . Fit competition brake pads, front and rear Fit race master cylinder cap Permitted race cap option; it removes the low-fluid warning, so treat…","keywords":"This section is generated from your saved builder choices. Edit prep jobs . Fit competition brake pads, front and rear Fit race master cylinder cap Permitted race cap option; it removes the low-fluid warning, so treat it as post-handover race prep. Fit passenger side impact bar Fit half sidescreens Full sidescreens come with the car; half sidescreens are the common race-prep upgrade. Fit Caterham race mirror package Required when using half sidescreens. The car needs three or more effective mirrors; without full doors, fit left and right side mirrors plus a centre mirror. Fit Pitking centre mirror Choose optional steering wheel The Momo suede wheel is one option; other compliant wheels are allowed. Fit front anti-roll bar Fit Mocal fuel drain hose with stop tap Fit dash timer Simple Salter-style timer for stint/race timing. Fit additional foot camera mount Fit camera lens protection pair Apply 50 mm anti-slip tape to both impact bars You stand on the impact bar when climbing over the cage into the car. Use Edit prep jobs to add optional parts to this list."},{"title":"Controls, Data & Wiring","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#controls-data-wiring","summary":"Keep wiring changes deliberate. Some jobs are Service Centre work, and the inertia switch choice is one route or the other. Remove key barrel and fit ignition switch Post-handover race-prep job for replacing the key…","keywords":"Keep wiring changes deliberate. Some jobs are Service Centre work, and the inertia switch choice is one route or the other. Remove key barrel and fit ignition switch Post-handover race-prep job for replacing the key barrel with a dashboard ignition switch while retaining the red starter button. For a plain toggle, use down as OFF and up as ON. If fitting an aircraft-style guarded switch, decide whether you really want the guard to act as a quick ignition cut: it must need a positive action to close and must not be able to fall or bounce shut. Mark the OFF position before scrutineering. Rotate the transponder 180 degrees Protects the sensitive cable, especially if the car ends up in a gravel trap. Register the MyLaps TR2 transponder Register it with MyLaps before the first event so the timing system can identify the car. Fit inertia switch jumper Driver Guide recommends taking the inertia switch out of the equation because kerbs can trip it and stop the engine. Use Caterham jumper 370L0003A after IVA/handover."},{"title":"Factory Checks & Setup","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#factory-checks-setup","summary":"Confirm factory-prepped items, then finish the service and setup baseline before the first serious test. Secure hoses with cable ties Drill catch tank cap and fit oil-resistant sponge The catch tank cap is not drilled…","keywords":"Confirm factory-prepped items, then finish the service and setup baseline before the first serious test. Secure hoses with cable ties Drill catch tank cap and fit oil-resistant sponge The catch tank cap is not drilled as supplied. Drill the cap, remove the small seal if needed, put oil-resistant sponge in the cap, and cable-tie the hoses. Full brake fluid change/bleed after fitting brake pressure sensor Check tyre valve stems are short type Prevents long valves being damaged by wheel-to-wheel contact. Remove wheel centre caps Remove them immediately and keep them out of reach. They can fall out during racing, hit the car behind, or be reported as something falling off the car, which risks a black/orange flag. Driver Guide says cars should be supplied with caps removed after IVA and loose in a bag; check every wheel set. Remove front number plate before track use Driver Guide says front number plate removal is mandatory for track conditions because airflow to the intercooler matters. Check fire extinguisher before first event Remove the green plastic shipping seal, check the pin and gauge, then keep the pin fitted until you are ready to go on track. Check supplied windscreen-to-roll-cage tape Cars are now arriving with tape already fitted between the windscreen and front cage hoop. Check it is secure and only covers that gap; keep a spare roll of 50 mm gaffer tape in the event kit. Confirm fuel sampling kit status The ARDS presentation calls out a fuel sampling kit, but the current notes do not confirm whether it is supplied or already fitted. Confirm with Caterham before the first event. Check supplied boot-floor tape Cars are now arriving with the boot already taped. Check the tape is present and secure before the first event; do not add extra boot-floor sealing unless Caterham gives a specific reason. Adjust clutch pedal stop Set the stop so there is only a little extra pedal travel once the clutch is fully open, reducing the risk of bearing damage. Set pedals for heel-and-toe Adjust pedal position and spacing after the brakes have bedded in, so the throttle can be blipped while braking. Check engine seals are intact Leave the three plastic engine seals intact. If removal is needed, contact the eligibility scrutineer first. Check hot oil pressure behaviour Red at hot idle can be normal, but above 2000 rpm it should not stay in the red. On track the Driver Guide expects around 4-5 bar. Service with uprated engine oil, regular filter, and gearbox oil Apply low friction front bearing grease Book flat floor setup Do this after the main fitment work and before serious testing. Prep sequence"},{"title":"Start With The Factory Car, Then Prep After Delivery","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#start-with-the-factory-car-then-prep-after-delivery","summary":"The useful way to think about Academy prep is not \"what can I change?\" but \"what should I order, and when should it be fitted?\" The factory car is the baseline; the useful prep list is the set of post-handover parts,…","keywords":"The useful way to think about Academy prep is not \"what can I change?\" but \"what should I order, and when should it be fitted?\" The factory car is the baseline; the useful prep list is the set of post-handover parts, setup items, and checks to complete before testing. 1"},{"title":"Inspect The Handover Car","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#inspect-the-handover-car","summary":"Caterham handles IVA at the factory, so prep starts from the delivered road-compliant car. Confirm what has arrived, keep factory-supplied standard parts out of the shopping list, and use the full parts list only when…","keywords":"Caterham handles IVA at the factory, so prep starts from the delivered road-compliant car. Confirm what has arrived, keep factory-supplied standard parts out of the shopping list, and use the full parts list only when you need a regulation part-number check. 2"},{"title":"Fit The Useful Prep Parts","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#fit-the-useful-prep-parts","summary":"Work through the parts that make the car easier or safer to race: driver fit, visibility, brakes, data, impact protection, controls, and setup parts. Leave Service Centre-only jobs to Caterham or an appointed Caterham…","keywords":"Work through the parts that make the car easier or safer to race: driver fit, visibility, brakes, data, impact protection, controls, and setup parts. Leave Service Centre-only jobs to Caterham or an appointed Caterham Motorsport Service Centre. 3"},{"title":"Finish Driver Fit, Then Test","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#finish-driver-fit-then-test","summary":"Set pedals, seat, belts, mirrors, head restraint, and helmet clearance together before setup work. Once the car is fitted and shaken down, use Race prep and Spanner checks for the repeated event jobs. Quick reference","keywords":"Set pedals, seat, belts, mirrors, head restraint, and helmet clearance together before setup work. Once the car is fitted and shaken down, use Race prep and Spanner checks for the repeated event jobs. Quick reference"},{"title":"Prep Parts And Why They Matter","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#prep-parts-and-why-they-matter","summary":"Open this only when you need part numbers, sources, or fitment notes. For the complete regulation reference, use the full parts list . Open prep parts reference","keywords":"Open this only when you need part numbers, sources, or fitment notes. For the complete regulation reference, use the full parts list . Open prep parts reference"},{"title":"Order Separately, Fit After Handover","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#order-separately-fit-after-handover","summary":"Moulded FIA-FT3 fuel cell Part 300T0023A Benefit Alternative permitted fuel tank type; order separately and plan the later fitment. Timing Order from Caterham Parts; fit after handover. Fuel cell aluminium container…","keywords":"Moulded FIA-FT3 fuel cell Part 300T0023A Benefit Alternative permitted fuel tank type; order separately and plan the later fitment. Timing Order from Caterham Parts; fit after handover. Fuel cell aluminium container Required with fuel cell Part 300T0024A Benefit Required container when choosing the moulded FIA-FT3 fuel cell route. Timing Order from Caterham Parts; fit after handover. Passenger side impact protection bar Part 30P307E , superseding regulation-listed 30P307A Benefit Adds side impact protection on the passenger side; the hood is not compatible and is not race-permitted. The regulations list 30P307A, but 30P307E supersedes it. Order the side protection bar hardware pack as well. Timing Order from Caterham Parts; fit after handover. Side protection bar hardware pack Required with impact bar Part Hardware pack Benefit Required hardware pack when ordering the passenger side impact protection bar. Timing Order with 30P307E / 30P307A. Driver's side honeycomb panel Part 76817 Benefit Raises the driver's feet, which can help pedal position and comfort for some drivers. Timing Order separately; fit during seat and pedal setup. Fog/reverse light blanking cover Required if lights removed Part 300V0084A Benefit Permitted if removing the fog and reverse light units as a pair; blanking covers must be used to cover the exposed mounting holes if present. Timing Conscious post-handover bodywork modification, not a spare."},{"title":"Cockpit Protection And Visibility","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#cockpit-protection-and-visibility","summary":"Half sidescreen kit Part WE67K Benefit Common upgrade from the full sidescreens that come with the car; fit the race mirror package when using half sidescreens. Kit contents 76258A RH half sidescreen, 76257A LH half…","keywords":"Half sidescreen kit Part WE67K Benefit Common upgrade from the full sidescreens that come with the car; fit the race mirror package when using half sidescreens. Kit contents 76258A RH half sidescreen, 76257A LH half sidescreen, 76213 button x4, 76212 socket x4, 30Z1046B base x4, 76211M one-way base x4, 76211B one-way button x4, 76211 one-way socket x4, and 1604-0514 countersunk rivet x4. Timing Visibility and cockpit comfort setup. Custom bead seat fitting and covering Required if clearance fails Source Specialist service Benefit Improves driver support, can sit the driver lower, and can be required if washers under the seat do not give at least 5 cm helmet-to-roll-cage clearance. Check clearance to the top of the cage with the driver helmeted and belted in; the finished seat must not restrict arm or leg movement. Timing Before final harness, pedal, FHR, and helmet-clearance checks. Seat tilt washers for helmet clearance Source Workshop fitment Benefit First option if helmet-to-roll-cage clearance is under 5 cm: add washers under the seat to tilt the seat back. Use a straight edge across the top of the cage if the clearance is hard to judge. Timing During driver fit setup. Momo suede steering wheel, optional Part 30S038A Benefit Optional race-only steering wheel; other compliant steering wheels are allowed, so choose based on driver preference, rules, and reach. Timing Driver fit setup. Handbrake cover or tape Source Soft Bits handbrake cover Benefit Covers the handbrake/tunnel gap so wrist restraints do not catch on the handbrake; tape can do the same job, while the Soft Bits cover looks neater. Timing Before using wrist restraints. Seat-back foam filler/support insert Mandatory Source Factory or specialist fitment Benefit Fills the gap between the driver's seat back and the seat-back bulkhead; this is mandatory and should be rechecked after seat changes. Timing Delivery inspection and final driver fit. Caterham race mirror package Required with half sidescreens Source Race mirror package Benefit Required when using half sidescreens; the car needs three or more effective mirrors, so use left and right side mirrors plus a centre mirror when running without full doors. Mirrors remain subject to visibility requirements. Timing Fit with half sidescreens. Pitking centre mirror Source Pitking Caterham centre mirror Benefit Centre mirror option to improve rear visibility alongside the race mirror package. Timing Driver visibility setup. Windscreen-to-roll-cage tape Source 50 mm gaffer tape Benefit Now expected to be supplied between the windscreen and front hoop of the roll cage to reduce rain ingress. Keep the tape within that area: do not run it further backwards or down the side of the screen/cage. Timing Check at delivery; keep spare tape in the event kit. Impact bar anti-slip tape Source 50 mm anti-slip tape Benefit Applied to both impact bars because you stand on the bar when climbing over the cage into the car. Timing Fit after the impact bars are fitted."},{"title":"Fuel, Catch Tank, Brakes, And Data","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#fuel-catch-tank-brakes-and-data","summary":"Mocal fuel drain hose with stop tap Source Mocal fuel drain hose Benefit Useful for fuel draining; confirm installation route against the permitted dry-break/fuel-drain rules. Timing Fuel-system prep. Catch tank…","keywords":"Mocal fuel drain hose with stop tap Source Mocal fuel drain hose Benefit Useful for fuel draining; confirm installation route against the permitted dry-break/fuel-drain rules. Timing Fuel-system prep. Catch tank foam/sponge insert Source Foam/sponge insert Benefit Oil-resistant sponge in the drilled catch tank cap helps reduce oil being blown out. Timing Drill the cap and fit the sponge during catch-tank prep. Race master cylinder cap Part 77175 or 300B0043A Benefit Permitted race cap options; both remove the low-fluid warning, so this belongs in post-handover race prep. Timing After handover, before race use. Competition front brake pads Part 300B0067A Benefit Permitted front race pad option and useful spare before testing starts. Timing Before shakedown/testing. Competition rear brake pads Part 3BAB0007A Benefit Permitted rear race pad option and useful spare before testing starts. Timing Before shakedown/testing. RaceSense B Source RaceSense B Benefit Independent logger for throttle position, RPM, acceleration, rotation, coolant temperature, and air temperature. Timing Install independently before testing. Brake pressure sensor Source RaceSense brake pressure sensor Benefit Useful data channel for brake trace review; plan a full brake fluid change/bleed after fitting. Timing Before testing and data setup. Dash timer Source Salter kitchen timer Benefit Simple dash timer for stint, race, or session timing; countdown timer/stopwatch fitment is permitted. Timing Before first track sessions. MyLaps TR2 transponder registration Source MyLaps account/app Benefit Registers the supplied timing transponder to the car before competition; do this as well as rotating the transponder to protect the cable. Timing Before the first event."},{"title":"Camera Accessories","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#camera-accessories","summary":"Additional foot camera mount Source Camera accessory Benefit Adds a dedicated pedal/footwork camera angle for driver review. Timing Before camera layout is finalised. Camera lens protection pair Source Camera accessory…","keywords":"Additional foot camera mount Source Camera accessory Benefit Adds a dedicated pedal/footwork camera angle for driver review. Timing Before camera layout is finalised. Camera lens protection pair Source Camera accessory Benefit Protects exposed camera lenses from stones, debris, and paddock handling damage. Timing Fit with camera hardware."},{"title":"Setup Parts","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#setup-parts","summary":"Front anti-roll bar, 5/8 inch, red Part 72090 Benefit Permitted front ARB option for tuning balance. The Driver Guide notes the Academy car must run a front anti-roll bar; the orange and red options are chosen by…","keywords":"Front anti-roll bar, 5/8 inch, red Part 72090 Benefit Permitted front ARB option for tuning balance. The Driver Guide notes the Academy car must run a front anti-roll bar; the orange and red options are chosen by preference and conditions. Timing Before setup work. Quick release front anti-roll bar kit Part 30F063AK Benefit Makes front ARB changes quicker during setup work. Timing Before setup work. Reinforced De Dion tube Part 370R0003A Benefit Permitted reinforced rear suspension tube. Timing Before setup work. Race braze-welded A-frame Part 300R0004A Benefit Permitted alternative to the standard MIG-welded A-frame. Timing Before setup work. Camber shim 0.1mm / 0.1 deg Part 300R0005A Benefit Fine rear camber adjustment. Timing During geometry setup. Camber shim 0.2mm / 0.2 deg Part 300R0006A Benefit Fine rear camber adjustment. Timing During geometry setup. Camber shim 0.5mm / 0.5 deg Part 300R0007A Benefit Larger rear camber adjustment. Timing During geometry setup. Toe shim 0.5mm / 0.08 deg Part 300R0008A Benefit Fine rear toe adjustment. Timing During geometry setup. Toe shim 1.0mm / 0.16 deg Part 300R0009A Benefit Larger rear toe adjustment. Timing During geometry setup. Low friction front bearing grease Source Service consumable Benefit Service-prep item for reducing front hub bearing drag. Timing Before testing and setup work. Uprated engine oil and regular filter Source Service consumable Benefit Track-prep service item; use the preparer's chosen oil and normal filter spec. Timing Pre-season service. Uprated gearbox oil Source Service consumable Benefit Track-prep service item for gearbox protection under repeated circuit use. Timing Pre-season service. Flat floor setup Source Setup service Benefit Sets the baseline ride height, rake, corner weights, and alignment after the main fitment work is complete. Do it in race trim with driver weight, hot tyre pressures, and enough ride-height margin for tyre wear and scrutineering-bay variation. Timing After fitment, before serious testing. Short tyre valve stems Source Specify when ordering tyres Benefit Reduces the risk of long valves being damaged by wheel-to-wheel contact. Timing When sourcing Toyo Proxes CF2 tyres through HP Tyre."},{"title":"Data And Switches","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#data-and-switches","summary":"VBOX OBD adaptor lead Part 370D0003A Benefit Permitted OBD/CAN adaptor where relevant; RaceSense may use its own supplied lead. Timing Before data setup. ACES programmable sequential shift lights Caterham recommended…","keywords":"VBOX OBD adaptor lead Part 370D0003A Benefit Permitted OBD/CAN adaptor where relevant; RaceSense may use its own supplied lead. Timing Before data setup. ACES programmable sequential shift lights Caterham recommended Part ACES01 Benefit Recommended shift-light option; shift-light choice is free. Timing Driver feedback setup. Inertia switch relocation subloom Caterham recommended location Part 37E552A Choose this if You are relocating the standard fuel inertia cut-out switch rather than removing it. Benefit Extends the existing loom so the switch can be mounted on the centre tunnel, just in front of the passenger seat on the left-hand side of the gear lever, where it is easier to reset. Note This remains a permitted option, but the Driver Guide recommends the jumper/removal route. Inertia switch removal jumper Driver Guide recommended Part 370L0003A Choose this if You are following the Driver Guide recommendation to remove the standard fuel inertia cut-out switch after IVA/handover. Benefit Kerbs can trip the inertia switch and stop the engine suddenly. The jumper completes the circuit after switch removal; only this Caterham jumper may be used for that purpose. Do not also order The subloom, unless you later decide to relocate the switch instead. Immobiliser bypass Caterham caution Source Service Centre job Use only if You are having immobiliser issues; Caterham do not recommend the bypass as routine prep. Requirement Permitted only when carried out by Caterham or an appointed Caterham Motorsport Service Centre. Timing Post-handover troubleshooting, not a standard prep item. Remove key barrel and fit ignition switch Source Service Centre job Direction For a plain two-position toggle, install down as OFF and up as ON. Many aircraft-style guarded installs use cover down as OFF, so the guard acts as a quick ignition cut. That only makes sense if the cover has a positive action and cannot fall or bounce shut while driving. If you want protection against accidental switch-off instead, use a locking toggle or guarded switch layout that deliberately protects the ON position. The regulations require the ignition switch OFF position to be marked. Benefit Simplifies race switchgear and avoids key-barrel/steering-lock issues while keeping the existing starter button as the start control. Check After fitting, confirm the dashboard wakes up only with the ignition switch ON, the red starter button only cranks with ignition ON, the guard or toggle cannot be knocked to OFF accidentally, and the engine stops cleanly when the marked OFF position is selected. Timing Post-handover race prep."},{"title":"Roll Cage Protection","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#roll-cage-protection","summary":"Roll cage foam fitting and aero shaping Source Specialist service Benefit Remove the supplied tie wraps, shape the FIA-approved padding, trim the foam ends so it can be taped neatly into position, and secure it anywhere…","keywords":"Roll cage foam fitting and aero shaping Source Specialist service Benefit Remove the supplied tie wraps, shape the FIA-approved padding, trim the foam ends so it can be taped neatly into position, and secure it anywhere the helmet could contact the cage. The safety fit comes first. Timing Before final driver fit checks. Roll cage padding wrap Source Specialist service Benefit Neater roll-cage padding finish, usually matched to the car or cockpit colour scheme. Timing Before final driver fit checks."},{"title":"Spares","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#spares","summary":"Nylon rear wing screws Part BM5 x 25 Benefit Academy cars are now supplied with nylon rear wing fixings as standard. They let a rear wing detach more cleanly, which can reduce bodywork damage and make the wing easier to…","keywords":"Nylon rear wing screws Part BM5 x 25 Benefit Academy cars are now supplied with nylon rear wing fixings as standard. They let a rear wing detach more cleanly, which can reduce bodywork damage and make the wing easier to reuse or replace. Timing Useful spares once racing starts; for road use or trackdays, metal fixings may retain the wing better after light contact."},{"title":"Rules And Guardrails","section":"Delivery prep","url":"delivery-prep.html#rules-and-guardrails","summary":"Caterham may change part numbers during the life of the regulations. A later suffix that fully supersedes an earlier suffix may be used where the earlier suffix is listed. If a standard part is not identified in the…","keywords":"Caterham may change part numbers during the life of the regulations. A later suffix that fully supersedes an earlier suffix may be used where the earlier suffix is listed. If a standard part is not identified in the regulations by a specific part number, the standard part is the part number in the car's bill of materials or a later bill of materials for an identical specification car. Plan light removal, blanking covers, trim removal, or event-only bodywork changes separately from delivery prep. Use permitted Toyo Proxes CF2 tyres sourced through HP Tyre Ltd, Daventry. Avoid the specifically prohibited quicker ratio steering rack, part number 30S030A."},{"title":"Choose A Guide Section","section":"Driver guide","url":"driver-guide.html#choose-a-guide-section","summary":"Start with the basics, then use the smaller guide pages when you need more detail on safety kit, setup, events, rules, or racecraft. Basics & Terms Responsibilities, glossary, safety kit, tools, and the basic car notes.…","keywords":"Start with the basics, then use the smaller guide pages when you need more detail on safety kit, setup, events, rules, or racecraft. Basics & Terms Responsibilities, glossary, safety kit, tools, and the basic car notes. Car Prep Knowledge Driver fit, controls, bodywork, compliance, fuel, data, and ARDS confirm items. Harness & Safety Harness setup, FHR/HANS notes, arm restraints, and belting-in order. Setup & Maintenance Race setup basics, flat-floor process, oil checks, pads, bearings, and routine checks. Events & Rules Race-weekend process, sprint notes, first race, flags, Parc Ferme, and protests. Racecraft Qualifying, starts, overtaking, defending, the tow, driving tips, and marshalling. Sources & Coverage Where each PDF section is covered, what has been cross-checked, and what still needs confirmation. How to use it"},{"title":"Checklists Stay Separate","section":"Driver guide","url":"driver-guide.html#checklists-stay-separate","summary":"The guide pages explain the why and how. The operational pages are still the place to work through jobs at the car. 1","keywords":"The guide pages explain the why and how. The operational pages are still the place to work through jobs at the car. 1"},{"title":"Delivery Prep","section":"Driver guide","url":"driver-guide.html#delivery-prep","summary":"Post-handover fitment, parts to order, and first shakedown checks. Open Delivery prep 2","keywords":"Post-handover fitment, parts to order, and first shakedown checks. Open Delivery prep 2"},{"title":"Race Prep","section":"Driver guide","url":"driver-guide.html#race-prep","summary":"Event, session, driver-safety, road reset, and scrutineering checks. Open Race prep 3","keywords":"Event, session, driver-safety, road reset, and scrutineering checks. Open Race prep 3"},{"title":"Spanner Checks","section":"Driver guide","url":"driver-guide.html#spanner-checks","summary":"Torque values, support points, walkaround checks, tools, and consumables. Open Spanner checks","keywords":"Torque values, support points, walkaround checks, tools, and consumables. Open Spanner checks"},{"title":"Your Responsibilities","section":"Basics & terms","url":"guide-basics.html#your-responsibilities","summary":"The Academy is built around equal cars, tightly controlled rules, and novice drivers. Preparation and compliance are the driver's responsibility. Source: Driver Guide intro and regulations responsibility notes.","keywords":"The Academy is built around equal cars, tightly controlled rules, and novice drivers. Preparation and compliance are the driver's responsibility. Source: Driver Guide intro and regulations responsibility notes."},{"title":"What That Means","section":"Basics & terms","url":"guide-basics.html#what-that-means","summary":"Read the championship regulations, final instructions, and relevant NCR chapters before events. If a modification is not clearly permitted, assume it is not permitted. No professional team support is allowed at events;…","keywords":"Read the championship regulations, final instructions, and relevant NCR chapters before events. If a modification is not clearly permitted, assume it is not permitted. No professional team support is allowed at events; Caterham support is for advice and emergency help, not routine car preparation. Safety scrutineering checks that the car and kit are safe. Eligibility scrutineering checks that the car matches the regulations. The driver is ultimately responsible if the car is found non-compliant. Use Delivery prep , Race prep , and Spanner checks as operational checklists. Language"},{"title":"Key Terms","section":"Basics & terms","url":"guide-basics.html#key-terms","summary":"Terms the Driver Guide expects you to understand before race weekends start moving quickly. Source: Driver Guide glossary. Term What it means Assembly / collecting area Where cars gather before qualifying or a race. It…","keywords":"Terms the Driver Guide expects you to understand before race weekends start moving quickly. Source: Driver Guide glossary. Term What it means Assembly / collecting area Where cars gather before qualifying or a race. It is rarely the pit lane, so identify it from the final instructions. Clerk of the Course The event decision maker. You normally see the Clerk at briefings or if an incident needs discussion. FIA / Motorsport UK / NCR The FIA is the world governing body, Motorsport UK is the UK authority, and the National Competition Rules are the general UK motorsport rules. NCR references use chapter, appendix, and article numbers. Final instructions Event-specific instructions covering timings, locations, briefings, passes, assembly, and procedures. Read them carefully. FHR / HANS / Hybrid Frontal head restraint systems used with the helmet and harness to reduce head and neck movement in an impact. FHR use is mandatory. Heel-and-toe Blipping the throttle while braking and downshifting so engine speed matches wheel speed, reducing rear-wheel locking and instability. Judge of Fact An official whose judgement is treated as fact unless formally appealed with strong evidence. Timekeepers and scrutineers can be Judges of Fact. Parc Ferme Where cars go directly after qualifying or races for eligibility checks. Stay with the car until released by the scrutineer. Regs / regulations The sporting and technical rules. In Academy conversation, \"the regs\" usually means the technical rules. Scrutineer An official checking safety or eligibility. If they find a problem, the driver has to put it right or may not be able to compete. Testing A circuit practice day for race licence holders in competition cars. Trackday overtaking conventions do not apply. Timekeepers / TSL Officials and timing systems that produce qualifying times, grids, race times, and live timing, usually available through TSL Timing at race events. Track The hard surface between the track limits. Kerbs count where permitted, but going beyond the white line or far edge of the kerb can be penalised. Setup terms Camber, caster, rake, ride height, tracking/toe, corner weights, and flat-floor setup describe how the car is adjusted to suit the driver and conditions. Before the season"},{"title":"Safety Kit And Tools","section":"Basics & terms","url":"guide-basics.html#safety-kit-and-tools","summary":"The Academy package includes the car safety hardware, but the driver still needs compliant personal kit and basic tools for the season. Source: Driver Guide shopping list and tools section.","keywords":"The Academy package includes the car safety hardware, but the driver still needs compliant personal kit and basic tools for the season. Source: Driver Guide shopping list and tools section."},{"title":"Personal Safety Kit","section":"Basics & terms","url":"guide-basics.html#personal-safety-kit","summary":"Mandatory: helmet, FHR, race suit, gloves, race boots, and balaclava. Recommended: fireproof underwear, including top and long johns or shorts. Motorcycle helmets are not permitted. Helmet approvals listed in the Driver…","keywords":"Mandatory: helmet, FHR, race suit, gloves, race boots, and balaclava. Recommended: fireproof underwear, including top and long johns or shorts. Motorcycle helmets are not permitted. Helmet approvals listed in the Driver Guide include FIA 8860-2010, FIA 8859-2015, FIA 8860-2018, FIA 8860-2018-ABP, FIA 8859-2024, FIA 8859-2024-ABP, SNELL SA2015, SNELL SA2020, and SNELL SA2025. SNELL SA2015 is not valid after 31 December 2026. For events, pack a clear visor if you normally use a tinted visor; officials must be able to see your eyes."},{"title":"Essential Tools And Supplies","section":"Basics & terms","url":"guide-basics.html#essential-tools-and-supplies","summary":"Torque wrench capable of more than 85 Nm and a 19 mm deep socket. Jack and axle stands, using the correct support points from the spanner-check page. Tyre pressure gauge and tyre pump or inflator. Jerry can or cans,…","keywords":"Torque wrench capable of more than 85 Nm and a 19 mm deep socket. Jack and axle stands, using the correct support points from the spanner-check page. Tyre pressure gauge and tyre pump or inflator. Jerry can or cans, plus a funnel or spout. Some forecourts may require permission before filling jerry cans. Bucket, sponge, car shampoo, leather, and polish. Engine oil, coolant, brake fluid, spare wheel, cable ties, tape, cleaning fluids, and the consumables listed on Spanner checks . The car"},{"title":"Getting To Know The Car","section":"Basics & terms","url":"guide-basics.html#getting-to-know-the-car","summary":"Basic operating and inspection points from the Driver Guide before you start changing setup. Source: Driver Guide getting-to-know-your-car section.","keywords":"Basic operating and inspection points from the Driver Guide before you start changing setup. Source: Driver Guide getting-to-know-your-car section."},{"title":"Starting, Running, And Instruments","section":"Basics & terms","url":"guide-basics.html#starting-running-and-instruments","summary":"Start with the key and red start button. Shut down with the key. Do not press the red start button while the engine is running, because it engages the starter motor. For the first 500 miles, avoid labouring the engine…","keywords":"Start with the key and red start button. Shut down with the key. Do not press the red start button while the engine is running, because it engages the starter motor. For the first 500 miles, avoid labouring the engine at low revs or repeatedly using very high revs. The ECU rev limiter is 6600 rpm and must not be altered with an external unit. Hot idle oil pressure can read in the red, but pressure should rise immediately with throttle. On track, expect around 4-5 bar. If oil pressure stays in the red above 2000 rpm, check the oil immediately or speak to Caterham."},{"title":"Seats, Hood, Engine Seals, And Heater","section":"Basics & terms","url":"guide-basics.html#seats-hood-engine-seals-and-heater","summary":"Choose a seat that lets the helmeted driver sit at least 50 mm below the top of the roll cage. The standard race seat may need supplied washers under the front runners to change rake. The gap behind the seat must be…","keywords":"Choose a seat that lets the helmeted driver sit at least 50 mm below the top of the roll cage. The standard race seat may need supplied washers under the front runners to change rake. The gap behind the seat must be filled once the seat position is settled. A custom foam or bead seat can improve comfort, support, and helmet clearance. A hood is useful for paddock rain and can be used on some test days, but it is not permitted for racing. Leave the three engine seals intact. If access requires removal, contact the eligibility scrutineer first. The car has a heated windscreen but no heater, and a heater cannot be fitted."},{"title":"Driver Fit And Controls","section":"Car preparation","url":"guide-car-prep.html#driver-fit-and-controls","summary":"Spend time making the car comfortable before competition; this pays back when you need to focus on driving. Source: Driver Guide car prep and ARDS helmet-clearance slides.","keywords":"Spend time making the car comfortable before competition; this pays back when you need to focus on driving. Source: Driver Guide car prep and ARDS helmet-clearance slides."},{"title":"Fit And Pedals","section":"Car preparation","url":"guide-car-prep.html#fit-and-pedals","summary":"Adjust pedals, seat, belts, mirrors, head restraint, steering reach, and helmet clearance together. Adjust the clutch pedal stop so there is only a little extra travel once the clutch is fully open. Set pedals so you…","keywords":"Adjust pedals, seat, belts, mirrors, head restraint, steering reach, and helmet clearance together. Adjust the clutch pedal stop so there is only a little extra travel once the clutch is fully open. Set pedals so you can heel-and-toe. The brake pedal may sit closer than the throttle when static; do final pedal setup after the brakes have bedded in. Helmet clearance is measured to the top of the cage, not the underside of the bars. Use a straight edge across the cage if needed. If clearance is short, tilt the seat back first. If that is not enough, use a bead seat. Set the head restraint as close as practical to the helmet/FHR in the normal belted-in driving position. Bodywork and visibility"},{"title":"Bodywork, Mirrors, And Tape","section":"Car preparation","url":"guide-car-prep.html#bodywork-mirrors-and-tape","summary":"Source: Driver Guide preparing-your-car section and ARDS race-car-conversion slides.","keywords":"Source: Driver Guide preparing-your-car section and ARDS race-car-conversion slides."},{"title":"Competition Prep Details","section":"Car preparation","url":"guide-car-prep.html#competition-prep-details","summary":"Remove wheel centre caps immediately and check every wheel set. Loose caps can hit following cars or trigger a black/orange flag report. Academy cars are supplied with nylon rear wing fixings as standard. They make wing…","keywords":"Remove wheel centre caps immediately and check every wheel set. Loose caps can hit following cars or trigger a black/orange flag report. Academy cars are supplied with nylon rear wing fixings as standard. They make wing loss cleaner but can let wings detach more easily after light contact. In wet conditions, use a single strip of tape between the windscreen and front cage hoop only; do not extend it further back or down the screen/cage. Rain-X or similar products are commonly used on the windscreen and visor, inside and outside, but may make wipers smear. The car must have three or more mirrors. Without doors, use left and right side mirrors plus a centre mirror. Sprint mirror requirements can differ because nobody is racing behind you. Headlight tape is an NCR requirement to help keep broken glass together. Use tape across the glass and about 50 mm onto the headlight bowl; avoid red and blue. Cover the handbrake/tunnel gap with tape or a cover so arm restraints do not catch, while keeping the handbrake functional. Numbers that matter"},{"title":"Compliance And Scrutineering Risks","section":"Car preparation","url":"guide-car-prep.html#compliance-and-scrutineering-risks","summary":"Source: 2026 regulations, Driver Guide, and ARDS scrutineering slides.","keywords":"Source: 2026 regulations, Driver Guide, and ARDS scrutineering slides."},{"title":"Before The First Event","section":"Car preparation","url":"guide-car-prep.html#before-the-first-event","summary":"Minimum weight is 635 kg including the driver in full racing kit. Drivers under about 85 kg should plan to check ballast needs. Minimum ride height is 140 mm. Leave margin for tyre wear and scrutineering bay variation.…","keywords":"Minimum weight is 635 kg including the driver in full racing kit. Drivers under about 85 kg should plan to check ballast needs. Minimum ride height is 140 mm. Leave margin for tyre wear and scrutineering bay variation. Race decals must be fitted to the supplied plan and kept correct through the season. Race numbers must be covered, removed, or crossed out before road use. Remove the front number plate before competition or track use because intercooler airflow matters. The Driver Guide recommends removing the fuel inertia switch after IVA/handover using the supplied Caterham jumper, because kerbs can trip the switch and stop the engine. Remove the tie wraps from the supplied FIA roll-cage padding, shape and trim the foam ends, then tape it neatly into position anywhere the helmet could contact the cage. Torque wheel nuts to 85 Nm / 65 lb ft before every event, test session, and after working on the car. Do not over-torque them. For the extinguisher, remove the green shipping seal before the first event. Keep the pin in for scrutineering, out for track sessions and Parc Ferme, and refit it when not in use. Fuel and timing"},{"title":"Fuel, Transponder, And ARDS Confirm Items","section":"Car preparation","url":"guide-car-prep.html#fuel-transponder-and-ards-confirm-items","summary":"Source: Driver Guide fuel/transponder notes and ARDS fuel/boot-floor slides.","keywords":"Source: Driver Guide fuel/transponder notes and ARDS fuel/boot-floor slides."},{"title":"Fuel And Data","section":"Car preparation","url":"guide-car-prep.html#fuel-and-data","summary":"Use commercially available pump fuel only. The HORSE HR13 mapping was done on 95 RON regular unleaded, so super unleaded gives no performance advantage. Do not use fuel additives or illegal fuel; the Driver Guide warns…","keywords":"Use commercially available pump fuel only. The HORSE HR13 mapping was done on 95 RON regular unleaded, so super unleaded gives no performance advantage. Do not use fuel additives or illegal fuel; the Driver Guide warns that penalties can be severe. Bring fuel because circuit pumps may be shut and sprint venues may not have fuel. Register the MyLaps TR2 transponder before the first event and check its LED before sessions. The ARDS presentation calls out a fuel sampling kit and boot floor sealing; confirm the current requirement with Caterham before the first event."},{"title":"Going Racing And Sprinting","section":"Events & rules","url":"guide-events-rules.html#going-racing-and-sprinting","summary":"Race weekends run to tight timetables. Prepare early and arrive with the car already ready. Source: Driver Guide going-racing section and ARDS rules slides.","keywords":"Race weekends run to tight timetables. Prepare early and arrive with the car already ready. Source: Driver Guide going-racing section and ARDS rules slides."},{"title":"The Week Before","section":"Events & rules","url":"guide-events-rules.html#the-week-before","summary":"Clean cockpit, engine bay, suspension, wheels, and bodywork. Spanner check the car and check brake pad wear. Check sponsor decals, number backgrounds, race numbers, and scrutineering stickers. If driving the race car to…","keywords":"Clean cockpit, engine bay, suspension, wheels, and bodywork. Spanner check the car and check brake pad wear. Check sponsor decals, number backgrounds, race numbers, and scrutineering stickers. If driving the race car to the event, cross out race numbers, remove tape from lights, inflate tyres 3-4 psi above normal pressure, and check lights. If towing, check trailer tyres, wheel nuts, brakes, number plate, tie-down straps, and lights. Pack the Caterham steering wheel, ignition key, and electrical cut-off key."},{"title":"Documents And Kit Bag","section":"Events & rules","url":"guide-events-rules.html#documents-and-kit-bag","summary":"Pack race licence and race upgrade card where required. Pack race suit, underwear, balaclava, gloves, arm restraints, boots, helmet, and FHR. Carry championship regulations and downloaded NCR Chapters 2, 5, 7, 9, and…","keywords":"Pack race licence and race upgrade card where required. Pack race suit, underwear, balaclava, gloves, arm restraints, boots, helmet, and FHR. Carry championship regulations and downloaded NCR Chapters 2, 5, 7, 9, and 12. Carry circuit passes, final instructions, tickets, and the race-car registration document if road driving. Take tools, tyre pump, pressure gauge, cleaning kit, jerry can, funnel, engine oil, coolant, brake fluid, and at least one spare wheel."},{"title":"At The Event","section":"Events & rules","url":"guide-events-rules.html#at-the-event","summary":"Arrive early and park in the allocated paddock area. Sign on as early as possible, online or in person as instructed. If issued, take the scrutineering slip to the scrutineering bay. For safety scrutineering, take suit,…","keywords":"Arrive early and park in the allocated paddock area. Sign on as early as possible, online or in person as instructed. If issued, take the scrutineering slip to the scrutineering bay. For safety scrutineering, take suit, gloves, boots, helmet, and FHR as required. Clean the car before scrutineering if it has been driven to the circuit. Attend briefings, including first-time-driver briefings, and read any briefing notes. Between scrutineering and qualifying or practice, check wheel nuts, tyre pressures, and fluid levels. For qualifying, arrive at assembly early, fully dressed, belted correctly, and with arm restraints ready. Complete three laps to qualify before pushing hard. After each event, make notes and set a checking programme before the next meeting. First competition"},{"title":"Sprint Guidance","section":"Events & rules","url":"guide-events-rules.html#sprint-guidance","summary":"The sprint is one car at a time, against the clock, with less traffic but the same need for preparation. Source: Driver Guide sprint guidance.","keywords":"The sprint is one car at a time, against the clock, with less traffic but the same need for preparation. Source: Driver Guide sprint guidance."},{"title":"Sprint Day Notes","section":"Events & rules","url":"guide-events-rules.html#sprint-day-notes","summary":"Do as much car preparation as possible before travelling; the morning will disappear quickly. Read all event paperwork, supplementary regulations, and final instructions. Have documents, helmet, and overalls ready for…","keywords":"Do as much car preparation as possible before travelling; the morning will disappear quickly. Read all event paperwork, supplementary regulations, and final instructions. Have documents, helmet, and overalls ready for scrutineering. Bring enough fuel for the day, but remember that excess fuel adds weight. Drink fluids and manage energy; the day is more tiring than it looks. Listen for paddock announcements and be ready for runs early. At the sprint start, green means the course is clear and you may go when ready. The clock starts when you break the timing beam, so do not creep. Watch your 64 ft times as a guide to start quality. Watch how other drivers start and approach the course. Use the sprint to compete against yourself as well as others; analyse runs and look for time. Ask questions early if unsure. First race weekend"},{"title":"Testing, Timetable, And Race Sessions","section":"Events & rules","url":"guide-events-rules.html#testing-timetable-and-race-sessions","summary":"The first race adds testing, qualifying traffic, starts, race procedure, and Parc Ferme discipline. Source: Driver Guide first-race section and ARDS pre-session checks.","keywords":"The first race adds testing, qualifying traffic, starts, race procedure, and Parc Ferme discipline. Source: Driver Guide first-race section and ARDS pre-session checks."},{"title":"Testing","section":"Events & rules","url":"guide-events-rules.html#testing","summary":"Testing is for competition licence holders in competition cars. Trackday overtaking rules do not apply. Book pre-race test days early because places are limited. Use testing to learn the track, try setup changes, and…","keywords":"Testing is for competition licence holders in competition cars. Trackday overtaking rules do not apply. Book pre-race test days early because places are limited. Use testing to learn the track, try setup changes, and practise without treating the session as a race. Be aware that faster and heavier cars may overtake aggressively under braking on open test days."},{"title":"Race Weekend Discipline","section":"Events & rules","url":"guide-events-rules.html#race-weekend-discipline","summary":"Read final instructions. Each circuit can have different procedures. Make a note of all relevant times and locations. Timetables can run ahead. Do not be late. Missing qualifying can mean starting at the back or not…","keywords":"Read final instructions. Each circuit can have different procedures. Make a note of all relevant times and locations. Timetables can run ahead. Do not be late. Missing qualifying can mean starting at the back or not racing. For self-declaration and safety checks, check rain light, brake lights, windscreen cracks, handbrake cover, clear visor, extinguisher pin procedure, transponder LED, weight, ride height, helmet clearance, and numbers. Qualifying is normally 15 minutes and races are normally 20 minutes, with boards for five minutes to go and last lap. Watch mirrors and stay aware of other cars at all times. Under a safety car, close the field safely into a compact line. When the safety car lights go out and it pulls in, the leader controls the pace and there is no overtaking until after the start line and green signals. After qualifying or racing, go directly to Parc Ferme and stay with the car until released. Only drivers, scrutineers, and authorised personnel should be in Parc Ferme unless permitted. Rules on track"},{"title":"Rules, Flags, Protests, And Standards","section":"Events & rules","url":"guide-events-rules.html#rules-flags-protests-and-standards","summary":"The Driver Guide highlights these because they frequently catch new drivers out. Source: Driver Guide rules section, NCR references, and ARDS rules slides.","keywords":"The Driver Guide highlights these because they frequently catch new drivers out. Source: Driver Guide rules section, NCR references, and ARDS rules slides."},{"title":"Flags And Procedures","section":"Events & rules","url":"guide-events-rules.html#flags-and-procedures","summary":"Blue flag: a faster car is approaching. Use mirrors and be predictable. White flag: slow vehicle ahead. Yellow flag: slow down, be prepared for danger, and do not overtake until after the green flag. Yellow and red…","keywords":"Blue flag: a faster car is approaching. Use mirrors and be predictable. White flag: slow vehicle ahead. Yellow flag: slow down, be prepared for danger, and do not overtake until after the green flag. Yellow and red striped flag: change of surface conditions, such as oil, coolant, debris, or water. Red flag: stop racing or qualifying immediately, but slow progressively, check mirrors, and signal if safe. In qualifying go to the pit lane unless instructed otherwise; in a race stop where marshals direct you and do not leave the circuit. If a race is red-flagged, results or restart order may be counted back to the last completed timing point. If the red flag is on the first lap, expect the original grid to be reformed unless officials say otherwise. Green flag: racing or overtaking resumes only after you pass the flag. Black/white diagonal flag: driving standards warning if shown with your number. Black/orange disc flag: mechanical issue; enter the pit lane and stop as instructed if shown with your number. Black flag: enter the pit lane and stop as instructed if shown with your number. Do not assume a black flag is for you unless your number is displayed. Chequered flag: the session is over; do not slow abruptly and do not pass it more than once."},{"title":"Track Limits And Judicial Process","section":"Events & rules","url":"guide-events-rules.html#track-limits-and-judicial-process","summary":"Stay on the track. A single wheel beyond the line or far side of a kerb can count as leaving the track. Qualifying track-limits offences can delete the relevant lap time. Race track-limits escalation in the Driver Guide…","keywords":"Stay on the track. A single wheel beyond the line or far side of a kerb can count as leaving the track. Qualifying track-limits offences can delete the relevant lap time. Race track-limits escalation in the Driver Guide is: first offence treated as misjudgement, second offence black/white flag, third offence five-second penalty, fourth offence drive-through, fifth offence exclusion. If an incident needs protest, notify the Clerk within 30 minutes of results being published and follow the formal NCR process. Useful video evidence can matter. If penalised, appeal through the Secretary of the Meeting within the permitted time and with the required fee. Check the current NCR for the fee amount. Only the Clerk and officials can take formal action; telling Caterham personnel is not a substitute for the formal process. If called to see the Clerk, use the championship handout or notes in the car to stay calm and follow the process. Safe and courteous driving is the driver's responsibility. Racing is non-contact. Driving Standards Improvement coaches are there to improve driving standards, not to help drivers win. On trackdays, behave as an Academy ambassador. Do not use racecraft aggressively against regular trackday customers."},{"title":"Qualifying Tips","section":"Racecraft","url":"guide-racecraft.html#qualifying-tips","summary":"Think about qualifying rather than just driving as fast as possible for the whole session. Source: Driver Guide qualifying advice.","keywords":"Think about qualifying rather than just driving as fast as possible for the whole session. Source: Driver Guide qualifying advice."},{"title":"Session Approach","section":"Racecraft","url":"guide-racecraft.html#session-approach","summary":"Relax, get ready early, and avoid starting the session in a panic. Know the cars and drivers slightly faster than you so you can learn from them if they appear ahead. Let clearly faster cars past predictably; stay on…","keywords":"Relax, get ready early, and avoid starting the session in a panic. Know the cars and drivers slightly faster than you so you can learn from them if they appear ahead. Let clearly faster cars past predictably; stay on line and signal awareness rather than jumping out of the way. Find space. Do not turn qualifying into a race in a group of cars. A fast lap starts at the last corner of the previous lap and ends only after crossing the line. Consider weather: if rain is coming, early laps may matter; if it is drying, keep going. Use the whole session. The first laps are banker laps to warm car, tyres, brakes, and driver. Racecraft"},{"title":"Race Starts And Racing","section":"Racecraft","url":"guide-racecraft.html#race-starts-and-racing","summary":"Source: Driver Guide race-starts, overtaking, and defending sections.","keywords":"Source: Driver Guide race-starts, overtaking, and defending sections."},{"title":"Starts And Overtaking","section":"Racecraft","url":"guide-racecraft.html#starts-and-overtaking","summary":"After the green-flag lap, form on the grid, watch for the five-second board and red lights, then go when the lights go out. React to the lights, not the car ahead. Use enough revs for a clean launch, but aim for only a…","keywords":"After the green-flag lap, form on the grid, watch for the five-second board and red lights, then go when the lights go out. React to the lights, not the car ahead. Use enough revs for a clean launch, but aim for only a small amount of wheelspin; left-foot clutch control matters most. Do not try to win the race at the first corner. You normally have enough time to create safer opportunities. Overtake where you can keep speed afterwards, not just where you can briefly out-brake someone. Sometimes staying second until the final tow opportunity is smarter than passing too early. Pressure the car ahead by being visible in mirrors, but do not drive unpredictably or weave. Defend the inside line only when needed; defensive lines are slower and can invite an outside or cutback move. If you make a mistake, stay focused and keep racing. Others will also make mistakes. Driving"},{"title":"The Tow And General Driving Tips","section":"Racecraft","url":"guide-racecraft.html#the-tow-and-general-driving-tips","summary":"Source: Driver Guide tow explanation and driving tips.","keywords":"Source: Driver Guide tow explanation and driving tips."},{"title":"The Tow","section":"Racecraft","url":"guide-racecraft.html#the-tow","summary":"The Caterham creates a useful low-pressure area behind it. The following car can gain speed in the tow on long full-throttle sections. To use the tow, line up behind the car ahead, stay there until close, then pull out…","keywords":"The Caterham creates a useful low-pressure area behind it. The following car can gain speed in the tow on long full-throttle sections. To use the tow, line up behind the car ahead, stay there until close, then pull out smoothly and firmly to pass. Be ready for the tow to pull you into the car ahead if you misjudge closing speed. To break a tow, the lead car can move once early, then return to the racing line if needed. Weaving is not allowed."},{"title":"Driving Tips","section":"Racecraft","url":"guide-racecraft.html#driving-tips","summary":"Apply the brakes progressively; do not stamp on them. Warm the tyres before driving quickly, especially the fronts. Do not light up the rear tyres leaving the pit lane; it can create front/rear temperature imbalance and…","keywords":"Apply the brakes progressively; do not stamp on them. Warm the tyres before driving quickly, especially the fronts. Do not light up the rear tyres leaving the pit lane; it can create front/rear temperature imbalance and understeer. Use steering inputs economically because every input costs speed. At first, learn the track and your driving before blaming the car or chasing setup changes. After gaining experience, experiment with tyre pressures, rake, geometry, and setup to suit your style. Trackdays and tuition are useful practice, but testing and racing have different rules and traffic behaviour. There is no substitute for time in the car, on road and track. To finish first, first you have to finish. Season knowledge"},{"title":"Marshalling, Contacts, And Mindset","section":"Racecraft","url":"guide-racecraft.html#marshalling-contacts-and-mindset","summary":"Source: Driver Guide marshalling, contacts, and end-of-guide notes.","keywords":"Source: Driver Guide marshalling, contacts, and end-of-guide notes."},{"title":"Marshalling","section":"Racecraft","url":"guide-racecraft.html#marshalling-2","summary":"Marshals are volunteers and deserve respect and thanks. A wave at the end of sessions is appreciated. After a race, whether you have won or not, acknowledge the marshals on the slowing-down lap. Completing a marshalling…","keywords":"Marshals are volunteers and deserve respect and thanks. A wave at the end of sessions is appreciated. After a race, whether you have won or not, acknowledge the marshals on the slowing-down lap. Completing a marshalling day and getting the licence signature is worth ten championship points if done before the final event. Do it early in the season if possible; it helps you understand how race meetings work from the other side. Arrange marshalling through the championship contacts."},{"title":"Contacts To Keep Handy","section":"Racecraft","url":"guide-racecraft.html#contacts-to-keep-handy","summary":"Championship assistants and Head of Motorsport for Academy matters. Caterham Parts for permitted parts and paddock support parts. Approved Caterham Motorsport Service Centres for Service Centre-only jobs. HP Tyres for…","keywords":"Championship assistants and Head of Motorsport for Academy matters. Caterham Parts for permitted parts and paddock support parts. Approved Caterham Motorsport Service Centres for Service Centre-only jobs. HP Tyres for the mandatory Toyo Proxes CF2 tyre supply. Trailer supplier contacts if using a trailer. Use the latest official Driver Guide or championship communications for current names, phone numbers, and email addresses."},{"title":"Mindset","section":"Racecraft","url":"guide-racecraft.html#mindset","summary":"Preparation is part of racing. Do not leave checks until the day before the next event. Clean, presented cars are easier to inspect and less likely to be sent away by scrutineers. Most early gains come from driver…","keywords":"Preparation is part of racing. Do not leave checks until the day before the next event. Clean, presented cars are easier to inspect and less likely to be sent away by scrutineers. Most early gains come from driver understanding, calmness, practice, and consistency rather than chasing a perfect setup."},{"title":"Harness Adjustment","section":"Harness & safety","url":"guide-safety.html#harness-adjustment","summary":"Do the adjustment work before events, not in a panic at the first race morning. Source: Driver Guide preparing-yourself section and ARDS harness demonstration slides.","keywords":"Do the adjustment work before events, not in a panic at the first race morning. Source: Driver Guide preparing-yourself section and ARDS harness demonstration slides."},{"title":"Adjustment Notes","section":"Harness & safety","url":"guide-safety.html#adjustment-notes","summary":"Pre-adjust crotch, lap, and shoulder straps before events. Lap straps must be very tight and worn across the pelvis, not the lower stomach. Crotch straps should be firm against the legs, routed up through the seat,…","keywords":"Pre-adjust crotch, lap, and shoulder straps before events. Lap straps must be very tight and worn across the pelvis, not the lower stomach. Crotch straps should be firm against the legs, routed up through the seat, across the inner thigh, through the D ring on the lap strap, and back towards the buckle. HANS/FHR users should have the shoulder strap adjusters on the FHR yoke area and use the inner upper harness mounting positions. If shoulder straps are too long, adjust the wrap behind the seat and lock the webbing correctly. Roll excess webbing and secure it so it cannot flap. In the car"},{"title":"Belting-In Order","section":"Harness & safety","url":"guide-safety.html#belting-in-order","summary":"Source: Driver Guide belting-in sequence and ARDS safety-equipment slides.","keywords":"Source: Driver Guide belting-in sequence and ARDS safety-equipment slides."},{"title":"Sequence","section":"Harness & safety","url":"guide-safety.html#sequence","summary":"Place arm restraints on your lap or passenger side ready to use. Plug in and tighten the lap straps first. Feed the crotch straps through the seat and lap-strap D rings. Put each arm-restraint loop onto the relevant…","keywords":"Place arm restraints on your lap or passenger side ready to use. Plug in and tighten the lap straps first. Feed the crotch straps through the seat and lap-strap D rings. Put each arm-restraint loop onto the relevant shoulder-strap clasp. Pass the shoulder strap through the crotch-strap loop and plug it into the buckle. Fit the arm restraints around the wrists, just above watch position. Tighten the shoulder straps firmly. If the lap straps are tight enough, the buckle should not climb up the chest. Driver safety"},{"title":"Arm Restraint Checks","section":"Harness & safety","url":"guide-safety.html#arm-restraint-checks","summary":"Source: Driver Guide arm-restraint guidance and ARDS arm-restraint slide.","keywords":"Source: Driver Guide arm-restraint guidance and ARDS arm-restraint slide."},{"title":"Fit And Reach","section":"Harness & safety","url":"guide-safety.html#fit-and-reach","summary":"Wear arm restraints on track; do not just leave them in the car. Set them so fingertips can just touch the inside of the roll cage, but hands cannot pass the cage. Make sure all controls and the centre mirror remain…","keywords":"Wear arm restraints on track; do not just leave them in the car. Set them so fingertips can just touch the inside of the roll cage, but hands cannot pass the cage. Make sure all controls and the centre mirror remain reachable. The left-hand restraint is usually longer than the right. Mark the shorter right-hand strap. Wear the loop on the wrist, not the forearm or upper arm. Check they cannot catch on the handbrake, tunnel, or other cockpit fittings."},{"title":"Race Setup Basics","section":"Setup & maintenance","url":"guide-setup-maintenance.html#race-setup-basics","summary":"Setup should be done in race trim, with the driver or equivalent driver weight in the seat and tyres at hot running pressures. Source: Driver Guide race-setup section and 2026 regulations.","keywords":"Setup should be done in race trim, with the driver or equivalent driver weight in the seat and tyres at hot running pressures. Source: Driver Guide race-setup section and 2026 regulations."},{"title":"Main Setup Areas","section":"Setup & maintenance","url":"guide-setup-maintenance.html#main-setup-areas","summary":"The Academy car must run a front anti-roll bar. The orange and red options are chosen by preference, driver style, and conditions. The thicker front anti-roll bar can shift grip balance toward rear traction but may…","keywords":"The Academy car must run a front anti-roll bar. The orange and red options are chosen by preference, driver style, and conditions. The thicker front anti-roll bar can shift grip balance toward rear traction but may unload the inside front tyre. Set front ride height no lower than 5 mm above the 140 mm minimum at the regulation reference point. Set rake after front ride height. Rear ride height is measured near the front edge of the rear wing; add the chosen rake amount to the front measurement. Set the right rear spring seat about 5 mm higher than the left as a starting point to compensate for driver offset. Set camber after ride height. Front camber is adjusted at the top ball joint; rear camber uses shims. Start caster in the middle lower-wishbone rear mounting position, then adjust with experience. Set tracking last because camber changes affect toe. Toe-out is normal on the front of an Academy car. Flat floor"},{"title":"Flat-Floor Procedure","section":"Setup & maintenance","url":"guide-setup-maintenance.html#flat-floor-procedure","summary":"Source: Driver Guide flat-floor setup order.","keywords":"Source: Driver Guide flat-floor setup order."},{"title":"Order Of Work","section":"Setup & maintenance","url":"guide-setup-maintenance.html#order-of-work","summary":"Put the car in race trim. Put the driver in race trim, or add equivalent weight. Check scale pad evenness with the driver. Remove all fuel. Weigh the car. Add ballast if required. Refill with half race-fuel capacity.…","keywords":"Put the car in race trim. Put the driver in race trim, or add equivalent weight. Check scale pad evenness with the driver. Remove all fuel. Weigh the car. Add ballast if required. Refill with half race-fuel capacity. Set tyre pressures. Check and adjust caster. Check and adjust ride heights. Check rake. Check and adjust camber. Recheck ride heights. Set corner weights. Check and adjust tracking. Straighten the steering wheel. Check ride heights again and repeat as needed. Between events"},{"title":"Maintenance And Checks","section":"Setup & maintenance","url":"guide-setup-maintenance.html#maintenance-and-checks","summary":"The Driver Guide repeats the spanner-check list because regular checking is what keeps the car reliable through race weekends. Source: Driver Guide maintenance section and Spanner Checks PDF.","keywords":"The Driver Guide repeats the spanner-check list because regular checking is what keeps the car reliable through race weekends. Source: Driver Guide maintenance section and Spanner Checks PDF."},{"title":"Core Maintenance Points","section":"Setup & maintenance","url":"guide-setup-maintenance.html#core-maintenance-points","summary":"Use the Spanner checks page for torque values and physical nut-and-bolt checks. Check front suspension, rear suspension, steering, engine, gearbox, wings, nose cone, lights, cooling, wheels, tyres, brake pads, and pedal…","keywords":"Use the Spanner checks page for torque values and physical nut-and-bolt checks. Check front suspension, rear suspension, steering, engine, gearbox, wings, nose cone, lights, cooling, wheels, tyres, brake pads, and pedal feel. The air filter usually needs no maintenance for the first 30,000 miles unless the car has run in dusty conditions. Check oil with the engine warm, above 60 degrees C, then switched off. Wipe the dipstick, refit it, wait 20 seconds, then read it. Add oil in small quantities, restart, run briefly, and recheck to avoid overfilling. Keep oil at the maximum mark with the engine hot. Make sure the dipstick is fully pushed home and secured so it cannot come out on track. Check front wheel bearings for minimum play without being too tight. Check rear brake pads regularly and inspect the inner pad carefully, as it is likely to wear first. For extended track use, fit the approved Caterham competition front and rear pads listed in the regulations. Keep the car clean enough that faults, leaks, loose parts, and scrutineering issues are easy to see."},{"title":"What are you working on?","section":"Caterham Academy","url":"index.html#choose-a-job","summary":"Get the car ready Plan your prep, choose the parts to fit and work through your delivery checklist. Delivery prep & checklist builder Prepare for a race Paperwork, kit, safety and the checks to make before and after…","keywords":"Get the car ready Plan your prep, choose the parts to fit and work through your delivery checklist. Delivery prep & checklist builder Prepare for a race Paperwork, kit, safety and the checks to make before and after each session. Race weekend checklist Check the car Work around the car with torque settings, tool sizes and mechanical checks. Workshop checklist"},{"title":"At the circuit","section":"Caterham Academy","url":"index.html#at-the-circuit","summary":"Before going out Car & driver checks Wheel nuts & session checks Torque & fluids Get belted in Harness, FHR & restraints Back in the paddock After-session checks","keywords":"Before going out Car & driver checks Wheel nuts & session checks Torque & fluids Get belted in Harness, FHR & restraints Back in the paddock After-session checks"},{"title":"Look something up","section":"Caterham Academy","url":"index.html#look-something-up","summary":"Parts & regulations Part numbers & options Setup & maintenance Setup, oil & brakes Events & rules Timetables & procedures Explore the driver guide All six chapters Checklists and prep choices are saved in this browser.…","keywords":"Parts & regulations Part numbers & options Setup & maintenance Setup, oil & brakes Events & rules Timetables & procedures Explore the driver guide All six chapters Checklists and prep choices are saved in this browser. This independent guide supports the current regulations and official Caterham instructions."},{"title":"Part Number Rule","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#part-number-rule","summary":"Caterham may change part numbers during the life of the regulations. A later suffix that fully supersedes an earlier suffix may be used where the earlier suffix is listed. If a standard part is not identified in the…","keywords":"Caterham may change part numbers during the life of the regulations. A later suffix that fully supersedes an earlier suffix may be used where the earlier suffix is listed. If a standard part is not identified in the regulations by a specific part number, the standard part is the part number in the car's bill of materials or a later bill of materials for an identical specification car. Safety and cockpit"},{"title":"Chassis / Safety","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#chassis-safety-2","summary":"Modification / option Part number Notes Suitable towing strap 30P344A Mandatory Towing straps are mandatory front and rear; choice of strap is free. Driver's side honeycomb panel 76817 Permitted to raise the driver's…","keywords":"Modification / option Part number Notes Suitable towing strap 30P344A Mandatory Towing straps are mandatory front and rear; choice of strap is free. Driver's side honeycomb panel 76817 Permitted to raise the driver's feet. No Caterham Parts product page found/listed."},{"title":"Permitted without a part number","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#permitted-without-a-part-number","summary":"Cut slots in the seat back aluminium panel for shoulder straps, with protection. Modify seatbelt/harness apertures to avoid strap contact. Fit a cool-suit system in the boot space, with two 15mm flameproofed pipe holes.…","keywords":"Cut slots in the seat back aluminium panel for shoulder straps, with protection. Modify seatbelt/harness apertures to avoid strap contact. Fit a cool-suit system in the boot space, with two 15mm flameproofed pipe holes. Modify upper seat belt anchorages for FHR suitability, but only via Caterham Westbury or Arch Motor & Manufacturing Ltd. Panels, screens, badges"},{"title":"Bodywork","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#bodywork-2","summary":"Modification / option Part number Notes Locking bonnet catches 30Z1079A Mandatory Rear bonnet fixing. Nylon rear wing screws BM5 x 25 Regs describe these as plastic; Caterham Parts lists them as nylon. Academy cars are…","keywords":"Modification / option Part number Notes Locking bonnet catches 30Z1079A Mandatory Rear bonnet fixing. Nylon rear wing screws BM5 x 25 Regs describe these as plastic; Caterham Parts lists them as nylon. Academy cars are now supplied with nylon rear wing fixings as standard. They let a wing detach more cleanly and can reduce bodywork damage, but the wing can also come off more easily after light cone, bollard, or car contact. For road use or trackdays, metal fixings may retain the wing better. Standard carbon-effect sidescreen, right-hand side 30V110A Carbon-effect option. Standard carbon-effect sidescreen, left-hand side 30V111A Carbon-effect option. Half sidescreen kit WE67K Permitted alternative. Kit contains 76258A RH half sidescreen, 76257A LH half sidescreen, 76213 button x4, 76212 socket x4, 30Z1046B base x4, 76211M one-way base x4, 76211B one-way button x4, 76211 one-way socket x4, and 1604-0514 countersunk rivet x4. Carbon-effect half sidescreen, right-hand side 30V086A Permitted alternative. Carbon-effect half sidescreen, left-hand side 30V087A Permitted alternative. Carbon-effect vinyl boot cover, regulation-listed number 300V0131A Regulations list this part number. Carbon-effect vinyl boot cover, current product page 30V089A Caterham Parts product page lists this part number. Plain windscreen glass 76050 Permitted instead of the standard heated glass. Stainless steel sill protectors 30P071A Permitted option, but usually unnecessary when using half sidescreens because entry is over the cage and impact bar rather than across the sill. Carbon fibre sill protectors 300P0236A Permitted option rather than a recommended prep purchase; Caterham Parts currently lists this as 300P0236B. Passenger side impact protection bar 30P307E , superseding regulation-listed 30P307A Hood is not compatible with this bar and is not permitted for racing. Regulations list 30P307A; 30P307E supersedes it. Order the side protection bar hardware pack as well. Side protection bar hardware pack Hardware pack Required with impact bar Required hardware pack when ordering the passenger side impact protection bar. Replacement wingstay, part 370W30003A 370W30003A Replacement for standard wingstays with side repeater wire hole. No Caterham Parts product page found/listed. Replacement wingstay, part 370W30004A 370W30004A Replacement for standard wingstays with side repeater wire hole. No Caterham Parts product page found/listed. Lower wingstay, left-hand side 300P0276A Alternative replacement without the side repeater wire hole. Lower wingstay, right-hand side 300P0277A Alternative replacement without the side repeater wire hole. Standard wingstay being replaced, left-hand side 76103 Listed as the standard wingstay with side repeater wire hole. Standard wingstay being replaced, right-hand side 76104 Listed as the standard wingstays with side repeater wire hole. \"CATERHAM\" rear badge 300V0053A Must be in the standard position, or the badge may be removed completely."},{"title":"Permitted without a part number","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#permitted-without-a-part-number-2","summary":"Mandatory track prep Remove the front number plate during competition or track use for intercooler airflow; rear number plates may also be removed. Use tape from the windscreen back to the front hoop of the roll cage to…","keywords":"Mandatory track prep Remove the front number plate during competition or track use for intercooler airflow; rear number plates may also be removed. Use tape from the windscreen back to the front hoop of the roll cage to reduce rain ingress. Use free-choice mirrors, subject to NCR visibility requirements. For sprint only, temporarily remove external mirrors/sidescreens. Remove passenger seat and passenger harness. Modify the centre arm rest/tunnel top by removing padding and/or deforming the substrate for elbow room. Use paint protection film on the leading edge of rear wings. Run without sidescreens entirely, subject to mirror requirements. Remove rubber trim and nut covers associated with IVA. Use tape temporarily to secure damaged wings/nose cones at events. Paint or vinyl-wrap the bodywork; foil wraps are allowed but may need reflection mitigation. With scrutineer permission, paint/repaint/anodise visible components for presentation. Adjust ride height subject to the minimum ground clearance rule. Powertrain"},{"title":"Engine / Cooling / Exhaust / Fuel Delivery","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#engine-cooling-exhaust-fuel-delivery","summary":"Modification / option Part number Notes Mesh \"7\" grille 3FP471D Mandatory Cooling-system fitment. Mesh \"7\" grille, alternative part number 30C093A Mandatory Cooling-system fitment. Cold air induction cut-out grille…","keywords":"Modification / option Part number Notes Mesh \"7\" grille 3FP471D Mandatory Cooling-system fitment. Mesh \"7\" grille, alternative part number 30C093A Mandatory Cooling-system fitment. Cold air induction cut-out grille 3FP623B Mandatory Induction-system fitment. Cold air induction cut-out grille, alternative part number 30P159C Mandatory Induction-system fitment. Exhaust silencer 3BAX0001B Only permissible exhaust system part. Not yet listed on Caterham Parts. Link pipe, turbo to cat 3BAX0004 Caterham Parts currently lists this as 3BAX0004A. Link pipe, cat to silencer 3BAX0002A Only permissible exhaust system part. Cat assembly 3BAX0003A Only permissible exhaust system part."},{"title":"Permitted without a part number","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#permitted-without-a-part-number-3","summary":"Drill the exhaust at the mating between the front link pipe and silencer and insert one rivet to stop silencer rotation. Caterham caution Bypass or remove the engine immobiliser only if you are having immobiliser…","keywords":"Drill the exhaust at the mating between the front link pipe and silencer and insert one rivet to stop silencer rotation. Caterham caution Bypass or remove the engine immobiliser only if you are having immobiliser issues; Caterham do not recommend this as routine prep, and it must be done by Caterham or an appointed Caterham Motorsport Service Centre. Fit FIA-approved dry-break connections to fuel lines to ease engine removal/replacement. Add wiring and a switch to run the fuel pump without ignition for fuel draining, when draining via a dry-break connection. Setup and hardware"},{"title":"Suspension","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#suspension-2","summary":"Modification / option Part number Notes Standard De Dion tube 79028B Permitted. Reinforced De Dion tube 370R0003A Permitted. Race braze-welded A-frame 300R0004A Permitted instead of standard MIG-welded A-frame. Standard…","keywords":"Modification / option Part number Notes Standard De Dion tube 79028B Permitted. Reinforced De Dion tube 370R0003A Permitted. Race braze-welded A-frame 300R0004A Permitted instead of standard MIG-welded A-frame. Standard MIG-welded A-frame 30R019B Standard part referenced by the rules. Camber shim 0.1mm / 0.1 deg 300R0005A Caterham Parts currently lists this as 300R0005B. Camber shim 0.2mm / 0.2 deg 300R0006A Caterham Parts currently lists this as 300R0006B. Camber shim 0.5mm / 0.5 deg 300R0007A Caterham Parts currently lists this as 300R0007B. Toe shim 0.5mm / 0.08 deg 300R0008A Caterham Parts currently lists this as 300R0008B. Toe shim 1.0mm / 0.16 deg 300R0009A Caterham Parts currently lists this as 300R0009C. Front anti-roll bar, 5/8 inch, red 72090 Permitted Caterham supplied front ARB. The Driver Guide says the car must not be run without a front anti-roll bar. Front anti-roll bar, 1/2 inch, orange 72088 Standard permitted Caterham supplied front ARB. The Driver Guide says the car must not be run without a front anti-roll bar. Quick release front anti-roll bar kit 30F063AK Permitted for easier front ARB changes."},{"title":"Permitted without a part number","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#permitted-without-a-part-number-4","summary":"Adjust ride height using standard damper spring seats. Adjust front camber to max 3 deg negative and castor via the adjustable top wishbone and lower wishbone shimming. Substitute the standard lower wishbone front…","keywords":"Adjust ride height using standard damper spring seats. Adjust front camber to max 3 deg negative and castor via the adjustable top wishbone and lower wishbone shimming. Substitute the standard lower wishbone front mounting bolt with a longer bolt of similar specification. Remove white nylon anti-squeak washers from the central-rear De Dion/A-frame mounting point. Modify front/rear damper spring seats by removing metal only for greater spring-position adjustment. Add circlip grooves to damper bodies for ride-height adjustment. Switches, logging, lights"},{"title":"Electrics / Data / Lighting","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#electrics-data-lighting","summary":"Modification / option Part number Notes Inertia switch subloom 37E552A Caterham recommended location Use this if relocating the standard fuel inertia cut-out switch rather than removing it. Caterham recommend mounting…","keywords":"Modification / option Part number Notes Inertia switch subloom 37E552A Caterham recommended location Use this if relocating the standard fuel inertia cut-out switch rather than removing it. Caterham recommend mounting the relocated switch on the centre tunnel, just in front of the passenger seat on the left-hand side of the gear lever, where it is easier to reset. The Driver Guide recommends the jumper/removal route. Inertia switch jumper wire 370L0003A Driver Guide recommended Use this if removing the standard fuel inertia cut-out switch rather than relocating it. Kerbs can trip the switch and stop the engine suddenly; the jumper completes the circuit after switch removal. Only this Caterham jumper may be used for that purpose. VBOX OBD adaptor lead 370D0003A Regulations state this is permitted when available; RaceSense may use the RaceSense supplied lead. Not yet listed on Caterham Parts. ACES programmable sequential shift lights ACES01 Caterham recommended Recommended by Caterham; shift-light choice is free. No Caterham Parts product page found/listed. Fog/reverse light blanking cover 300V0084A Required if lights removed Required if fog and reverse light units are removed. Fog/reverse light blanking insert 300V0086B Required if lights removed Required if fog and reverse light units are removed. No Caterham Parts product page found/listed. Clear front indicator lenses FRL425L Permitted with orange bulbs. Orange indicator bulbs B614 Permitted with clear front indicator lenses. High intensity rear light 30L284A Mandatory Rear warning light. Rear light clamp bracket RSGU132/25 Required with rear light Required bracket for rear warning light; do not drill or weld the roll cage."},{"title":"Permitted without a part number","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#permitted-without-a-part-number-5","summary":"Free-choice ignition/starter switch, positioned on the dashboard. For a plain two-position ignition toggle, install down as OFF and up as ON. If using an aircraft-style guarded switch, make sure its guard action is…","keywords":"Free-choice ignition/starter switch, positioned on the dashboard. For a plain two-position ignition toggle, install down as OFF and up as ON. If using an aircraft-style guarded switch, make sure its guard action is deliberate: it must not be able to fall or bounce shut and kill the ignition unintentionally. Mark the OFF position. Relocate or remove the standard fuel inertia cut-out switch, subject to loom/jumper rules above. Fit a countdown timer or stopwatch to the dash or steering wheel centre. Fit a non-integrated data-logging system and take a power feed and engine RPM signal. Connect RaceSense/VBOX/scrutineer-approved similar datalogging to OBD/CAN for engine data. Fit datalogging sensors such as throttle, brake and steering sensors, but not TPMS. Use standalone sport video systems independent from the car except power feed. Disconnect the wiring loom from the rear-wheel speed sensor. When replacing a cycle wing, omit refitting the side repeater. Fuel rules"},{"title":"Fuel","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#fuel","summary":"","keywords":""},{"title":"Required fuel source","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#required-fuel-source","summary":"Fuel rule Use commercially available pump fuel from normal forecourt pumps or circuit pumps. Do not use fuel additives or illegal fuels. The Driver Guide says the HORSE HR13 mapping was carried out on 95 RON regular…","keywords":"Fuel rule Use commercially available pump fuel from normal forecourt pumps or circuit pumps. Do not use fuel additives or illegal fuels. The Driver Guide says the HORSE HR13 mapping was carried out on 95 RON regular unleaded, so super unleaded gives no performance advantage. Pads and controls"},{"title":"Brakes","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#brakes-2","summary":"Modification / option Part number Notes Competition front brake pads 300B0067A Permitted replacement for standard front pads; use the current Caterham Parts number. Competition rear brake pads 3BAB0007A Permitted…","keywords":"Modification / option Part number Notes Competition front brake pads 300B0067A Permitted replacement for standard front pads; use the current Caterham Parts number. Competition rear brake pads 3BAB0007A Permitted replacement for standard rear pads. Race master cylinder cap 77175 or 300B0043A Not strictly road legal because these remove the low-fluid warning; treat as a post-factory-IVA race-prep change."},{"title":"Permitted without a part number","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#permitted-without-a-part-number-6","summary":"Use lockwire instead of standard R-clips to secure brake pad retaining pins. Use tape or another method to bridge the gap between handbrake lever end and transmission tunnel top. Driver controls","keywords":"Use lockwire instead of standard R-clips to secure brake pad retaining pins. Use tape or another method to bridge the gap between handbrake lever end and transmission tunnel top. Driver controls"},{"title":"Wheels / Steering","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#wheels-steering","summary":"Modification / option Part number Notes Apollo wheel, silver, regulation-listed number 30W044A Regulations list this part number. Apollo wheel, silver, current product page 30W029D Caterham Parts product page lists this…","keywords":"Modification / option Part number Notes Apollo wheel, silver, regulation-listed number 30W044A Regulations list this part number. Apollo wheel, silver, current product page 30W029D Caterham Parts product page lists this part number. Apollo wheel, black/polished, regulation-listed number 30W041A Regulations list this part number. Apollo wheel, black/polished, current product page 30W041D Caterham Parts product page lists this part number. Apollo wheel, Hi-Power silver 30W029A No Caterham Parts product page found/listed for the Hi-Power silver wheel. Quick release steering column 30S043A Permitted. Steering column UJ 300S0014A Permitted."},{"title":"Permitted without a part number","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#permitted-without-a-part-number-7","summary":"Paint wheels any colour. Free-choice steering wheel, subject to NCR 7.2.11. Fit a spacer between steering wheel and boss. Caterham recommended Remove or deactivate the steering lock; recommended by Caterham. Control tyre","keywords":"Paint wheels any colour. Free-choice steering wheel, subject to NCR 7.2.11. Fit a spacer between steering wheel and boss. Caterham recommended Remove or deactivate the steering lock; recommended by Caterham. Control tyre"},{"title":"Tyres","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#tyres-2","summary":"","keywords":""},{"title":"Permitted without a part number","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#permitted-without-a-part-number-8","summary":"Manually remove rubber pick-up from tyres.","keywords":"Manually remove rubber pick-up from tyres."},{"title":"Required tyre specification","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#required-tyre-specification","summary":"Mandatory tyre spec Toyo Proxes CF2, 185/60R13 front and rear. Tyres may only be sourced through HP Tyre Ltd, Daventry. Minimum weight","keywords":"Mandatory tyre spec Toyo Proxes CF2, 185/60R13 front and rear. Tyres may only be sourced through HP Tyre Ltd, Daventry. Minimum weight"},{"title":"Weight / Ballast","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#weight-ballast-2","summary":"","keywords":""},{"title":"Required minimum weight","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#required-minimum-weight","summary":"Mandatory Minimum weight is 635 kg, including the driver in full racing kit. Drivers weighing less than about 85 kg may need ballast to reach the minimum weight.","keywords":"Mandatory Minimum weight is 635 kg, including the driver in full racing kit. Drivers weighing less than about 85 kg may need ballast to reach the minimum weight."},{"title":"Permitted without a part number","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#permitted-without-a-part-number-9","summary":"Required if ballast fitted Add ballast if needed, fitted on the passenger side of the cockpit and mounted with at least four M10 fixings and suitable steel washers. Tank and filler options","keywords":"Required if ballast fitted Add ballast if needed, fitted on the passenger side of the cockpit and mounted with at least four M10 fixings and suitable steel washers. Tank and filler options"},{"title":"Fuel Tank","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#fuel-tank-2","summary":"Modification / option Part number Notes Standard aluminium tank 36T003A One permitted fuel tank type. Moulded FIA-FT3 fuel cell 300T0023A Alternative permitted fuel tank type. Aluminium honeycomb protection kit TI04P…","keywords":"Modification / option Part number Notes Standard aluminium tank 36T003A One permitted fuel tank type. Moulded FIA-FT3 fuel cell 300T0023A Alternative permitted fuel tank type. Aluminium honeycomb protection kit TI04P Mandatory with aluminium tank Required when the aluminium tank is retained. No Caterham Parts product page found/listed. Fuel cell aluminium container 300T0024A Required with fuel cell Required when using moulded fuel cell. Aero fuel filler, black 30T065A Mandatory with standard tank Required when standard tank is retained; cap tether may be removed. Aero fuel filler, silver 30T066A Mandatory with standard tank Required when standard tank is retained; cap tether may be removed."},{"title":"Permitted without a part number","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#permitted-without-a-part-number-10","summary":"Fit fuel-tank vent tube/additional check valve if needed to prevent spillage. With moulded fuel cell, retain redundant standard tank filler cap, blank the rear-panel hole with aluminium, or delete the hole during…","keywords":"Fit fuel-tank vent tube/additional check valve if needed to prevent spillage. With moulded fuel cell, retain redundant standard tank filler cap, blank the rear-panel hole with aluminium, or delete the hole during rear-panel replacement. Explicitly prohibited"},{"title":"Part Numbers To Avoid","section":"Parts & regulations","url":"parts.html#part-numbers-to-avoid","summary":"Prohibited item Part number Notes Quicker ratio steering rack 30S030A Specifically prohibited; intentionally not linked.","keywords":"Prohibited item Part number Notes Quicker ratio steering rack 30S030A Specifically prohibited; intentionally not linked."},{"title":"Paperwork, Kit, And Arrival Prep","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#paperwork-kit-and-arrival-prep","summary":"The Driver Guide is clear that race weekends run to tight timetables. Treat paperwork, kit, fuel, and presentation as part of race prep, not background admin.","keywords":"The Driver Guide is clear that race weekends run to tight timetables. Treat paperwork, kit, fuel, and presentation as part of race prep, not background admin."},{"title":"Week Before","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#week-before","summary":"Read the Supplementary Regulations and final instructions carefully and note briefing, sign-on, scrutineering, assembly, qualifying, and race times. Attend mandatory briefings and listen for paddock announcements; race…","keywords":"Read the Supplementary Regulations and final instructions carefully and note briefing, sign-on, scrutineering, assembly, qualifying, and race times. Attend mandatory briefings and listen for paddock announcements; race meeting timetables can run early. Download or carry the championship regulations and the relevant NCR chapters, especially Chapters 2, 5, 7, 9, and 12. Check circuit passes, entry tickets, paddock plan, and any car pass requirements. Check championship sponsor decals, number backgrounds, race numbers, and scrutineering stickers are correct and secure. Check decals are fitted to the supplied plan and replace any missing championship decals before scrutineering. Clean the car: cockpit, engine bay, suspension, wheels, and bodywork."},{"title":"Kit Bag And Documents","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#kit-bag-and-documents","summary":"Pack race licence. Pack upgrade card for race meetings where required. Pack race suit, gloves, boots, underwear, balaclava, helmet, and FHR. Check safety kit compliance against the Driver Guide safety kit notes . Pack a…","keywords":"Pack race licence. Pack upgrade card for race meetings where required. Pack race suit, gloves, boots, underwear, balaclava, helmet, and FHR. Check safety kit compliance against the Driver Guide safety kit notes . Pack a clear visor if you normally use a tinted visor. Pack arm restraints and make sure they are accessible before assembly. Pack the car registration document if driving the race car on the road. Take any scrutineering slip to the scrutineering bay if one is issued at sign-on."},{"title":"If Towing","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#if-towing","summary":"Check trailer tyre pressure and tread. Check trailer wheel nuts, brakes, number plate, tie-down straps, and lights. Pack the Caterham steering wheel, ignition key, and electrical cut-off key. Driver safety","keywords":"Check trailer tyre pressure and tread. Check trailer wheel nuts, brakes, number plate, tie-down straps, and lights. Pack the Caterham steering wheel, ignition key, and electrical cut-off key. Driver safety"},{"title":"Fit And Wear The Safety Kit Correctly","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#fit-and-wear-the-safety-kit-correctly","summary":"These are not just build checks. They need to be right every time the car goes on track.","keywords":"These are not just build checks. They need to be right every time the car goes on track."},{"title":"Harness, FHR, And Arm Restraints","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#harness-fhr-and-arm-restraints","summary":"Check the harness is routed correctly through the seat and around the FHR/HANS. Check shoulder straps do not run upwards from the shoulder slots; FHR/HANS use should be no more than 20 degrees below horizontal. Tighten…","keywords":"Check the harness is routed correctly through the seat and around the FHR/HANS. Check shoulder straps do not run upwards from the shoulder slots; FHR/HANS use should be no more than 20 degrees below horizontal. Tighten the lap straps first, then shoulder straps, so the harness holds you low in the seat. Wear arm restraints on track; do not just leave them fitted in the car. Check arm restraints cannot catch on the handbrake, tunnel, or other cockpit fittings. Confirm the seat-back void is filled and the seat foam/filler is still in place. If a bead seat has been fitted or adjusted, confirm it does not restrict arm or leg movement."},{"title":"Helmet And Cage","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#helmet-and-cage","summary":"Confirm the delivery-prep helmet-to-roll-cage clearance check has been completed. Before going on track, check the driver's helmet still has clear space to the roll cage when belted in. Check roll cage padding is fitted…","keywords":"Confirm the delivery-prep helmet-to-roll-cage clearance check has been completed. Before going on track, check the driver's helmet still has clear space to the roll cage when belted in. Check roll cage padding is fitted wherever the driver can contact the cage. Check roll cage padding is secure and has not moved during previous sessions. Session prep"},{"title":"Before Going Out","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#before-going-out-2","summary":"These are the quick checks to complete before joining the assembly area or leaving the paddock for a session.","keywords":"These are the quick checks to complete before joining the assembly area or leaving the paddock for a session."},{"title":"Car And Driver","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#car-and-driver","summary":"Torque the wheel nuts to 85 Nm / 65 lb ft. Check tyre pressures. Check all fluid levels are correct. Check brake pad material and pedal feel. Required visibility Check mirrors are fitted, secure, and provide the…","keywords":"Torque the wheel nuts to 85 Nm / 65 lb ft. Check tyre pressures. Check all fluid levels are correct. Check brake pad material and pedal feel. Required visibility Check mirrors are fitted, secure, and provide the required rear visibility. Mandatory Check tow straps are locked in position and accessible. Check camera equipment is secure if fitted. Check the transponder LED is illuminated. Check race numbers and sponsor decals are present and secure. Check the fire extinguisher gauge is in the green and the bottle, pipework, and pull handle are secure. Check the green plastic seal has been removed from the extinguisher pin so the pin can actually be pulled. Pull the fire extinguisher pin just before going out on track. Mandatory track prep Remove the front number plate before competition or track use for intercooler airflow."},{"title":"Lights And Tape","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#lights-and-tape","summary":"NCR requirement Apply headlight tape so it crosses the glass and helps stop broken glass scattering into small pieces. Electrical insulation tape generally works best. Black tape is fine; the issue is whether the tape…","keywords":"NCR requirement Apply headlight tape so it crosses the glass and helps stop broken glass scattering into small pieces. Electrical insulation tape generally works best. Black tape is fine; the issue is whether the tape actually crosses the glass and does the job. Extend the tape about 50 mm onto the headlight bowl so it is clearly anchored, not just sitting on the glass. Use diagonal \"times sign\" crosses rather than plus-sign crosses; they are usually easier to fit properly and look neater. Tape restriction Do not use red headlight tape. Blue has also been called out in Academy guidance, so avoid red and blue. Apply 50 mm gaffer tape from the windscreen back to the front hoop of the roll cage if needed to reduce rain ingress. Check rear warning light, brake lights, and indicators are secure and working where required. Paddock reset"},{"title":"When You Come Back In","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#when-you-come-back-in","summary":"Do the simple reset work immediately, while the session is fresh and before the next timetable pressure arrives.","keywords":"Do the simple reset work immediately, while the session is fresh and before the next timetable pressure arrives."},{"title":"After Session","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#after-session","summary":"Refit the fire extinguisher pin when you get back in. Check tyres for pressure, wear, and pickup. Check brake pad material and pedal feel again. Check for obvious fluid leaks, loose panels, or damaged bodywork. Review…","keywords":"Refit the fire extinguisher pin when you get back in. Check tyres for pressure, wear, and pickup. Check brake pad material and pedal feel again. Check for obvious fluid leaks, loose panels, or damaged bodywork. Review camera/data equipment while the session details are still fresh. Put the extinguisher pin somewhere safe in the car when it is out, and refit it when not in use. Event practicalities"},{"title":"Trackside Notes","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#trackside-notes-2","summary":"Small preparation items that help between sessions and during a race weekend.","keywords":"Small preparation items that help between sessions and during a race weekend."},{"title":"Useful Trackside Prep","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#useful-trackside-prep","summary":"Carry cable ties for the cycle wings in case one is lost or needs temporary securing. Use Rain-X on the windscreen and visor, inside and outside. Keep a short dash list for session reminders. If the windscreen takes…","keywords":"Carry cable ties for the cycle wings in case one is lost or needs temporary securing. Use Rain-X on the windscreen and visor, inside and outside. Keep a short dash list for session reminders. If the windscreen takes damage on the driver's side, plan how it will be rotated or replaced before further running. Carry pump fuel in jerry cans; some circuits have closed or unavailable pumps, and sprint venues may not have fuel. Carry a funnel or spout, engine oil, coolant, brake fluid, and at least one spare wheel. Carry cleaning kit: bucket, sponge, car shampoo, leather, and polish."},{"title":"Fuel Rules","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#fuel-rules","summary":"Fuel rule Use commercially available pump fuel from normal forecourt pumps or circuit pumps. Do not use illegal fuels or additives. The Driver Guide says the HORSE HR13 mapping was carried out on 95 RON regular…","keywords":"Fuel rule Use commercially available pump fuel from normal forecourt pumps or circuit pumps. Do not use illegal fuels or additives. The Driver Guide says the HORSE HR13 mapping was carried out on 95 RON regular unleaded, so super unleaded gives no performance advantage. Road use"},{"title":"Before Driving On The Road","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#before-driving-on-the-road","summary":"Some event markings and competition setup details make sense on track but need changing before road use.","keywords":"Some event markings and competition setup details make sense on track but need changing before road use."},{"title":"Road Reset","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#road-reset","summary":"Cover or remove race numbers before driving the car on the road. Remove tape from all lights before driving on the road. If driving to the event, inflate tyres 3-4 psi above normal pressure and check they are road…","keywords":"Cover or remove race numbers before driving the car on the road. Remove tape from all lights before driving on the road. If driving to the event, inflate tyres 3-4 psi above normal pressure and check they are road legal. Refit the fire extinguisher pin before leaving the track environment. Check all lights are working, especially rear fog and brake lights. Check lights, mirrors, and number plates are road-appropriate before road driving. Scrutineering risks"},{"title":"Numbers That Can End A Result","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#numbers-that-can-end-a-result","summary":"These are worth treating as measured checks, not vague reminders.","keywords":"These are worth treating as measured checks, not vague reminders."},{"title":"Measured Checks","section":"Race prep","url":"race-prep.html#measured-checks","summary":"Mandatory Check ground clearance is at least 140 mm at the exposed lower chassis side-rail reference point, with the driver in full racing kit seated normally. Mandatory Check minimum weight is at least 635 kg including…","keywords":"Mandatory Check ground clearance is at least 140 mm at the exposed lower chassis side-rail reference point, with the driver in full racing kit seated normally. Mandatory Check minimum weight is at least 635 kg including the driver and full racing kit. Required if ballast fitted If ballast is fitted, check it is on the passenger side and mounted with at least four M10 fixings and suitable steel washers. If the driver is under about 85 kg, plan to check whether ballast is likely to be needed. Check wheel centre caps are removed. Check the windscreen for cracks, especially in the driver's line of sight. Check the car is clean and presentable before scrutineering; dirty cars may be sent away to be cleaned before a ticket is issued. Do not present a wing held together with tape unless a scrutineer has already accepted it as a temporary event repair. Check race numbers are proper numbers, not made from tape. After qualifying or a race, go directly to Parc Ferme and stay with the car until released by the scrutineer."},{"title":"Reference Pages","section":"Reference library","url":"reference.html#reference-pages","summary":"Use these when you need the detail behind the operational checklists. Parts Reference Full permitted parts, options, part numbers, supersessions, and prohibited parts. Driver Guide Season process, safety, setup, events,…","keywords":"Use these when you need the detail behind the operational checklists. Parts Reference Full permitted parts, options, part numbers, supersessions, and prohibited parts. Driver Guide Season process, safety, setup, events, rules, racecraft, and background guidance. Search Search across workflow pages, guide pages, parts, and source coverage. Sources & Coverage Where the PDFs are covered and which items still need confirmation. Regulations PDF Open the published 20/03/2026 regulations copy from Caterham."},{"title":"How The Site Was Cross-Checked","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#how-the-site-was-cross-checked","summary":"The text-heavy PDFs were read directly and the ARDS deck was also reviewed visually from rendered slide contact sheets, because automated OCR did not return reliable text from the slide images.","keywords":"The text-heavy PDFs were read directly and the ARDS deck was also reviewed visually from rendered slide contact sheets, because automated OCR did not return reliable text from the slide images."},{"title":"Source Priority","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#source-priority","summary":"For technical legality, the 2026 regulations and later official bulletins override guide or presentation wording. Where a presentation visual showed older values, the site keeps the current regulation values, such as…","keywords":"For technical legality, the 2026 regulations and later official bulletins override guide or presentation wording. Where a presentation visual showed older values, the site keeps the current regulation values, such as 635 kg minimum weight. Names, phone numbers, and similar contact details are kept as categories only, because they can become stale quickly. Part-number links are practical buying references; always check current Caterham Parts supersession wording before ordering. Coverage"},{"title":"Where Each Source Section Is Covered","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#where-each-source-section-is-covered","summary":"This is the audit trail for the split pages. It is deliberately topic-based rather than a page-by-page copy of the PDFs.","keywords":"This is the audit trail for the split pages. It is deliberately topic-based rather than a page-by-page copy of the PDFs."},{"title":"Driver Guide pages 1-8","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#driver-guide-pages-1-8","summary":"Title, contents, intro, responsibility, and glossary. Covered in Driver guide hub and Basics & Terms . Driver responsibility for car compliance. Key Academy, NCR, race-weekend, and setup terminology.","keywords":"Title, contents, intro, responsibility, and glossary. Covered in Driver guide hub and Basics & Terms . Driver responsibility for car compliance. Key Academy, NCR, race-weekend, and setup terminology."},{"title":"Driver Guide pages 10-14","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#driver-guide-pages-10-14","summary":"Shopping list, personal kit, tools, and basic car operation. Covered in Safety kit and tools , car basics , and consumables . Helmet/FHR/suit kit, clear visor, and core tool list. Starting, running-in, oil pressure,…","keywords":"Shopping list, personal kit, tools, and basic car operation. Covered in Safety kit and tools , car basics , and consumables . Helmet/FHR/suit kit, clear visor, and core tool list. Starting, running-in, oil pressure, fuel gauge, heater, hood, and handbrake notes."},{"title":"Driver Guide pages 16-22","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#driver-guide-pages-16-22","summary":"Preparing the car for competition. Covered in Car Prep Knowledge , Delivery prep , and Parts Reference . Helmet clearance, seat fit, pedals, mirrors, numbers, tape, wheel caps, extinguisher, inertia switch, pads, and…","keywords":"Preparing the car for competition. Covered in Car Prep Knowledge , Delivery prep , and Parts Reference . Helmet clearance, seat fit, pedals, mirrors, numbers, tape, wheel caps, extinguisher, inertia switch, pads, and bodywork. Open confirmation items for fuel sampling and boot-floor sealing are kept visible below."},{"title":"Driver Guide pages 24-30","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#driver-guide-pages-24-30","summary":"Preparing yourself and fitting the harness. Covered in Harness & Safety and Race prep driver safety . FHR/HANS notes, inner upper harness mounting, strap routing, belting-in sequence, and arm-restraint checks.","keywords":"Preparing yourself and fitting the harness. Covered in Harness & Safety and Race prep driver safety . FHR/HANS notes, inner upper harness mounting, strap routing, belting-in sequence, and arm-restraint checks."},{"title":"Driver Guide pages 32-37","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#driver-guide-pages-32-37","summary":"Race setup and maintenance. Covered in Setup & Maintenance and Spanner checks . Ride height, rake, camber, caster, tracking, flat-floor order, oil checks, brake pads, wheel bearings, and cleaning.","keywords":"Race setup and maintenance. Covered in Setup & Maintenance and Spanner checks . Ride height, rake, camber, caster, tracking, flat-floor order, oil checks, brake pads, wheel bearings, and cleaning."},{"title":"Driver Guide pages 39-50","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#driver-guide-pages-39-50","summary":"Race weekends, sprinting, testing, first race, flags, and procedures. Covered in Events & Rules and Race prep . Week-before prep, sign-on, scrutineering, assembly, qualifying, Parc Ferme, sprint starts, flags, safety…","keywords":"Race weekends, sprinting, testing, first race, flags, and procedures. Covered in Events & Rules and Race prep . Week-before prep, sign-on, scrutineering, assembly, qualifying, Parc Ferme, sprint starts, flags, safety car, and red-flag procedure."},{"title":"Driver Guide pages 52-71","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#driver-guide-pages-52-71","summary":"Racecraft, driving tips, marshalling, contacts, and end notes. Covered in Racecraft and rules . Qualifying, starts, overtaking, defending, tow, driving tips, marshalling day, and contact categories. Current contact…","keywords":"Racecraft, driving tips, marshalling, contacts, and end notes. Covered in Racecraft and rules . Qualifying, starts, overtaking, defending, tow, driving tips, marshalling day, and contact categories. Current contact names and phone numbers are intentionally not copied into the site."},{"title":"ARDS slides 1-16","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#ards-slides-1-16","summary":"Safety equipment, harness fit, arm restraints, padding, and helmet clearance. Covered in Harness & Safety , driver fit , and driver safety . Harness/FHR fitment, arm restraints, seat-back void, cage padding, and 50 mm…","keywords":"Safety equipment, harness fit, arm restraints, padding, and helmet clearance. Covered in Harness & Safety , driver fit , and driver safety . Harness/FHR fitment, arm restraints, seat-back void, cage padding, and 50 mm helmet-to-cage clearance."},{"title":"ARDS slides 17-26","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#ards-slides-17-26","summary":"Road-car to race-car prep, headlight tape, windscreen tape, tow straps, and fuel sampling kit. Covered in bodywork and tape , before going out , and open questions . Headlight tape must hold the glass together and…","keywords":"Road-car to race-car prep, headlight tape, windscreen tape, tow straps, and fuel sampling kit. Covered in bodywork and tape , before going out , and open questions . Headlight tape must hold the glass together and extend onto the bowl; avoid red and blue. Fuel sampling kit status still needs confirmation."},{"title":"ARDS slides 27-38","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#ards-slides-27-38","summary":"Wheel centre caps, wheel nuts, rules, mirrors, camera, boot floor sealing, and extinguisher. Covered in bodywork , compliance , rules , and open questions . Wheel caps removed, race numbers not shown on road, three…","keywords":"Wheel centre caps, wheel nuts, rules, mirrors, camera, boot floor sealing, and extinguisher. Covered in bodywork , compliance , rules , and open questions . Wheel caps removed, race numbers not shown on road, three mirrors, camera fitment, extinguisher pin timing, and boot floor sealing query."},{"title":"ARDS slides 39-52","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#ards-slides-39-52","summary":"Jacking, axle stands, spanner checks, pads, exclusion risks, ride height, weight, and ballast. Covered in Spanner checks , compliance , weight and ballast , and brakes . Support points, no impact-gun wheel-nut…","keywords":"Jacking, axle stands, spanner checks, pads, exclusion risks, ride height, weight, and ballast. Covered in Spanner checks , compliance , weight and ballast , and brakes . Support points, no impact-gun wheel-nut tightening, 85 Nm wheel-nut torque, 140 mm ride height, competition pads, and ballast planning. The site uses the 635 kg regulation weight where the deck visual differs."},{"title":"Spanner Checks PDF","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#spanner-checks-pdf","summary":"Workshop nut-and-bolt checks, support points, top tips, tools, and consumables. Covered in Spanner checks and maintenance . Torque checks, support points, pre-session checks, top tips, no impact guns/wrenches, and…","keywords":"Workshop nut-and-bolt checks, support points, top tips, tools, and consumables. Covered in Spanner checks and maintenance . Torque checks, support points, pre-session checks, top tips, no impact guns/wrenches, and useful consumables."},{"title":"Sigma Assembly Guide, Edition 2.2","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#sigma-assembly-guide-edition-2-2","summary":"Supplementary tool-size cross-reference for the workshop checklist. Covered in Tool sizes and torque checks . Official Sigma assembly guide PDF ; references use printed page numbers. Used for tools and fastener…","keywords":"Supplementary tool-size cross-reference for the workshop checklist. Covered in Tool sizes and torque checks . Official Sigma assembly guide PDF ; references use printed page numbers. Used for tools and fastener illustrations only. The 2026 Academy checklist remains the torque source. Radius-arm compatibility confirmed by the owner. Other Sigma references require checking against the car; partial mappings and engine/gearbox differences are labelled."},{"title":"2026 Regulations","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#2026-regulations","summary":"Permitted modifications, mandatory items, weights, tyres, ballast, and part numbers. Covered in Parts Reference , delivery prep parts , and compliance . Full permitted-parts reference stays on the parts page; the prep…","keywords":"Permitted modifications, mandatory items, weights, tyres, ballast, and part numbers. Covered in Parts Reference , delivery prep parts , and compliance . Full permitted-parts reference stays on the parts page; the prep pages only carry practical buying and fitting choices. Confirm before acting"},{"title":"Open Questions","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#open-questions-2","summary":"These remain deliberately visible because the PDFs identify them, but the current supply or fitting position should be confirmed before treating them as jobs.","keywords":"These remain deliberately visible because the PDFs identify them, but the current supply or fitting position should be confirmed before treating them as jobs."},{"title":"Items To Confirm","section":"Sources & open questions","url":"sources.html#items-to-confirm","summary":"Fuel sampling kit: the ARDS deck calls it out, but confirm whether it is supplied, fitted, or only required to be available. Boot floor sealing: the ARDS deck shows this as a check item; confirm the reason and whether…","keywords":"Fuel sampling kit: the ARDS deck calls it out, but confirm whether it is supplied, fitted, or only required to be available. Boot floor sealing: the ARDS deck shows this as a check item; confirm the reason and whether the factory now handles it. Extinguisher visual checks: confirm the gauge, pin, and shipping seal before first use, then follow the pin-in/pin-out timing on the race prep page. Superseded part numbers: if Caterham Parts lists a later suffix as a full supersession, order the current replacement but keep the regulation-listed number in the audit trail."},{"title":"Torque Checks","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#torque-checks-2","summary":"Work through these checks before the car goes on track. The list is grouped by area so it can be used around the car in a sensible workshop order. Check the support points before working underneath; Sigma tool…","keywords":"Work through these checks before the car goes on track. The list is grouped by area so it can be used around the car in a sensible workshop order. Check the support points before working underneath; Sigma tool references must match the fasteners on your car."},{"title":"Front Suspension","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#front-suspension","summary":"Upper wishbones: 47 Nm. Sigma reference: 17 mm socket and spanner. Sigma pp. 8–9 . Lower wishbones: 81 Nm. Sigma reference: 19 mm socket and spanner. Sigma pp. 8–9 . Dampers: 25 Nm. Sigma reference: 6 mm Allen key / hex…","keywords":"Upper wishbones: 47 Nm. Sigma reference: 17 mm socket and spanner. Sigma pp. 8–9 . Lower wishbones: 81 Nm. Sigma reference: 19 mm socket and spanner. Sigma pp. 8–9 . Dampers: 25 Nm. Sigma reference: 6 mm Allen key / hex bit. Sigma pp. 12–13 . Top wishbone ball joints and locknuts: 54 Nm. Locknuts can only be hand-checked with a spanner. Partial Sigma reference: assembly lists 22 mm tools for ball-joint work; the locknut tool size is not separately identified. Sigma pp. 14–15 . Brake calipers to hubs: 60 Nm. Tool size unconfirmed: the Sigma guide supplies the front brake/upright assembly preassembled and does not specify this fixing’s tool. Sigma pp. 14–15 . Hubs to lower wishbones: 54 Nm. Partial Sigma reference: assembly lists ¾ inch and 22 mm tools, with different standard-track and wide-track fixings. Match the nut on your car. Sigma pp. 14–15 . Wingstays to uprights: 81 Nm. Sigma reference: assembly lists 19 mm / ¾ inch tools for the ½ inch fixing. Check the fit on your nut. Sigma pp. 14–15 . Wheel bearings: ensure wheels spin freely with no lateral play. Check the large centre nut with split pin."},{"title":"Rear Suspension","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#rear-suspension","summary":"Radius arms to chassis: 47 Nm. Tools: 8 mm Allen key / hex bit and 17 mm socket. Radius-arm compatibility confirmed by the owner; a shortened 8 mm Allen key helps access. Sigma pp. 106–107 . Radius arms to De Dion tube:…","keywords":"Radius arms to chassis: 47 Nm. Tools: 8 mm Allen key / hex bit and 17 mm socket. Radius-arm compatibility confirmed by the owner; a shortened 8 mm Allen key helps access. Sigma pp. 106–107 . Radius arms to De Dion tube: 81 Nm. Tools: 19 mm socket and spanner. Radius-arm compatibility confirmed by the owner. Sigma pp. 106–107 . De Dion tube aluminium endplate and bearing: 47 Nm. Sigma reference: 17 mm socket and spanners for the endplate / bearing-carrier fixings. Sigma pp. 114–115 . Check welds on both De Dion tube and radius arms for cracking or fatigue. Dampers to De Dion tube: 81 Nm. Sigma reference: 19 mm socket for the lower damper mounting bolt. Sigma pp. 104–105 . A-frame mountings, two outer and one centre: 81 Nm. Sigma reference: 19 mm socket and spanner; the guide lists two 19 mm sockets. Sigma pp. 108–109 . Differential cage to differential: 81 Nm cap-head bolts and 47 Nm M10 bolt. Tool sizes unconfirmed: the Sigma differential section does not clearly document the same cage-to-differential arrangement. Sigma pp. 96–97 . Differential cage to chassis: 81 Nm lower bolts and 54 Nm upper bolt. Sigma reference: 10 mm hex bit for the lower M12 cap-head bolts; 19 mm socket and spanner for the upper through-bolt. Verify the cage and fasteners match. Sigma pp. 96–97 . Check wheel bearings rotate smoothly."},{"title":"Steering","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#steering","summary":"Track rod ends to steering arms: 41 Nm. Tool size unconfirmed: the Sigma assembly tool list does not clearly identify this individual nut. Do not confuse it with the track-rod adjustment locknut. Sigma pp. 10–15 .…","keywords":"Track rod ends to steering arms: 41 Nm. Tool size unconfirmed: the Sigma assembly tool list does not clearly identify this individual nut. Do not confuse it with the track-rod adjustment locknut. Sigma pp. 10–15 . Steering rack clamp bolts: 11 Nm. Sigma reference: 5 mm Allen key / hex bit and 10 mm spanner for the M6 nuts. Sigma pp. 10–11 . Check lower-to-upper steering column clamp bolts and grub screw, with no play. Partial Sigma reference: the assembly lists a 7/16 inch spanner, 7/32 inch hex bit, 13 mm tools and an 11/16 inch socket. Individual tool assignments are not all clear; verify each fixing. Sigma pp. 88–89 . Check for no movement in the column bushes by moving the wheel up and down."},{"title":"Engine","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#engine","summary":"Engine mounting rubbers to chassis: 20 Nm. Sigma engine reference only: 13 mm tools for M8 fixings. Confirm against the 2026 engine installation. Sigma pp. 40–41 . Engine mounting rubbers to engine mounting brackets: 81…","keywords":"Engine mounting rubbers to chassis: 20 Nm. Sigma engine reference only: 13 mm tools for M8 fixings. Confirm against the 2026 engine installation. Sigma pp. 40–41 . Engine mounting rubbers to engine mounting brackets: 81 Nm. Sigma engine reference only: the assembly lists a ⅜ inch hex bit for the imperial cap-head fixing. Confirm against the 2026 engine installation. Sigma pp. 40–41 . Engine mounting brackets to engine block: 25 Nm M8 bolts and 47 Nm M10 bolts. Sigma engine reference only: 6 mm hex bit for M8 cap-head bolts; 8 mm hex bit for M10 cap-head bolts. Confirm against the 2026 engine installation. Sigma pp. 40–41 . Starter motor to engine: 47 Nm. Sigma engine reference only: 8 mm hex bit. Confirm the starter fixings on the 2026 engine. Sigma pp. 38–39 . Air filter: check it is not dusty or dirty. Bellhousing to engine: 47 Nm. Sigma engine reference only: 8 mm hex bit. Confirm the bellhousing fixings on the 2026 engine. Sigma pp. 38–39 . Check oil level with the engine warm, then switched off; wipe and refit the dipstick, wait 20 seconds, then read it. Ensure the dipstick is fully pushed home; the Driver Guide recommends securing it so it cannot come out on track. Check oil pressure behaviour: red at hot idle can be normal, but above 2000 rpm it should not stay in the red."},{"title":"Gearbox","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#gearbox","summary":"Gearbox mount to gearbox: 54 Nm. Sigma gearbox reference only: 22 mm socket for its five-speed installation, or 19 mm for its six-speed installation. Confirm the 2026 gearbox and mount match. Sigma pp. 42–43 . Gearbox…","keywords":"Gearbox mount to gearbox: 54 Nm. Sigma gearbox reference only: 22 mm socket for its five-speed installation, or 19 mm for its six-speed installation. Confirm the 2026 gearbox and mount match. Sigma pp. 42–43 . Gearbox mount to chassis: 20 Nm. Sigma gearbox reference only: 6 mm hex bit and 13 mm tools. Confirm the 2026 mount fixings. Sigma pp. 42–43 ."},{"title":"Before Every Session","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#before-every-session-2","summary":"Torque the wheel nuts before every session: 85 Nm or 65 lb ft. Tool: 19 mm deep socket and torque wrench, as listed in the 2026 Driver Guide (p. 11). Check oil level and wheel nuts again before going back on track.…","keywords":"Torque the wheel nuts before every session: 85 Nm or 65 lb ft. Tool: 19 mm deep socket and torque wrench, as listed in the 2026 Driver Guide (p. 11). Check oil level and wheel nuts again before going back on track. Start each weekend with enough brake pad material to last. If in doubt, change pads before the final practice session. Check the inner rear brake pads carefully; the Driver Guide notes they are likely to wear first. Workshop position"},{"title":"Jacking And Axle Stands","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#jacking-and-axle-stands","summary":"Get the car supported correctly before working through the spanner checks. These notes are about where the car is lifted and supported, not the torque values themselves.","keywords":"Get the car supported correctly before working through the spanner checks. These notes are about where the car is lifted and supported, not the torque values themselves."},{"title":"Support Points","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#support-points-2","summary":"Use the front towing eye as the front jacking point. Use a trolley jack at the rear in the centre under the A-frame. Use the correct front axle stand positions when spanner checking suspension. Use the correct rear axle…","keywords":"Use the front towing eye as the front jacking point. Use a trolley jack at the rear in the centre under the A-frame. Use the correct front axle stand positions when spanner checking suspension. Use the correct rear axle stand positions when spanner checking the rear of the car. Do not use stand positions marked as not suitable for suspension tightening. For suspension tightening checks, keep in mind that some checks are easier or more appropriate with the car on the floor. Choosing tools"},{"title":"Tool Sizes And Sources","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#tool-sizes-and-sources","summary":"Tool sizes appear alongside the checks below. Torque settings remain from the 2026 Academy checklist; the Sigma assembly guide is used only to cross-reference tools and fasteners. Sigma reference: match the illustrated…","keywords":"Tool sizes appear alongside the checks below. Torque settings remain from the 2026 Academy checklist; the Sigma assembly guide is used only to cross-reference tools and fasteners. Sigma reference: match the illustrated fastener to your car before using the listed tool. Radius-arm compatibility has been confirmed by the owner. Other Sigma references remain to be checked on the 2026 car, especially engine, bellhousing, starter and gearbox fittings. Partial reference: the guide lists tools for an assembly without identifying every individual fixing. Where the mapping is unclear, the check says so rather than assigning a size. Open the Sigma assembly guide, Edition 2.2 (PDF) . References below use printed page numbers; PDF viewer page numbers are nine higher. External PDFs require an internet connection. Keep the Academy torque values. For example, the Sigma radius-arm chassis drawing gives 34 Nm, while the Academy checklist gives 47 Nm. Do not substitute the Sigma torque when using its tool reference. M8 and M10 describe thread sizes, not socket or spanner sizes. Visual checks"},{"title":"Walkaround Checks","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#walkaround-checks","summary":"These are quick physical and visual checks to run alongside the spanner work.","keywords":"These are quick physical and visual checks to run alongside the spanner work."},{"title":"Lighting","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#lighting","summary":"Check all lights are working. Check all lights are fixed securely. Check lights are taped where required. Check rear bulbs are not touching the lenses.","keywords":"Check all lights are working. Check all lights are fixed securely. Check lights are taped where required. Check rear bulbs are not touching the lenses."},{"title":"Cooling System","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#cooling-system","summary":"Visually check hose clips and connections for leaks. Check coolant level visually. Check radiator mountings and condition visually.","keywords":"Visually check hose clips and connections for leaks. Check coolant level visually. Check radiator mountings and condition visually."},{"title":"Wheels And Tyres","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#wheels-and-tyres","summary":"Check tyre pressures. Check wheel weights are secure and taped.","keywords":"Check tyre pressures. Check wheel weights are secure and taped."},{"title":"Fibreglass","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#fibreglass","summary":"Check front wing fixings. Check rear wing fixings. Check nose cone fasteners. Check the grille is secure in the nose cone.","keywords":"Check front wing fixings. Check rear wing fixings. Check nose cone fasteners. Check the grille is secure in the nose cone."},{"title":"Other Checks","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#other-checks","summary":"Check race numbers and sponsor logos. Clean the car. Check brake pad material. Check that you have good pedal feel. Workshop notes","keywords":"Check race numbers and sponsor logos. Clean the car. Check brake pad material. Check that you have good pedal feel. Workshop notes"},{"title":"Top Tips","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#top-tips-2","summary":"Practical notes from the supplied checklist. Treat these as prompts to check against your own car and current championship guidance.","keywords":"Practical notes from the supplied checklist. Treat these as prompts to check against your own car and current championship guidance."},{"title":"Tools And Access","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#tools-and-access","summary":"Cut down an 8 mm Allen key for easier access on the radius arms. Do not use impact guns or impact wrenches. Use a trolley jack under the front towing eye and at the rear in the centre under the A-frame.","keywords":"Cut down an 8 mm Allen key for easier access on the radius arms. Do not use impact guns or impact wrenches. Use a trolley jack under the front towing eye and at the rear in the centre under the A-frame."},{"title":"Basic Workshop Kit","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#basic-workshop-kit","summary":"Low-profile trolley jack rated for at least 1.5 tonnes. Three axle stands so the car can be supported level for spanner checks. Torque wrench capable of more than 85 Nm, with a 19 mm deep socket for wheel nuts. Digital…","keywords":"Low-profile trolley jack rated for at least 1.5 tonnes. Three axle stands so the car can be supported level for spanner checks. Torque wrench capable of more than 85 Nm, with a 19 mm deep socket for wheel nuts. Digital tyre pressure gauge, ideally with a flexible hose. Tyre pump or inflator. Jerry can or cans with a funnel or spout. Bucket, sponge, car shampoo, leather, and polish. Small socket set and screwdriver set for trackside fixings."},{"title":"Consumables To Carry","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#consumables-to-carry","summary":"50 mm gaffer tape for the windscreen-to-roll-cage gap. Headlight tape, avoiding red and blue. 50 mm anti-slip tape for both impact bars. Cable ties for hoses, cycle wings, and temporary trackside fixes. Brake cleaner…","keywords":"50 mm gaffer tape for the windscreen-to-roll-cage gap. Headlight tape, avoiding red and blue. 50 mm anti-slip tape for both impact bars. Cable ties for hoses, cycle wings, and temporary trackside fixes. Brake cleaner and water for cleaning up brake fluid. Glass cleaner and Rain-X for the windscreen and visor, inside and outside. Workshop cloths, nitrile gloves, and disposable wipes. Engine oil, coolant, brake fluid, and a funnel or spout. Sweatband for the race master cylinder cap."},{"title":"Trackside Fixes","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#trackside-fixes","summary":"Carry cable ties for the cycle wings in case one is lost or needs temporary securing. Side repeaters are not required on race cars. If the windscreen is damaged on the driver's side, it can be rotated as a workaround…","keywords":"Carry cable ties for the cycle wings in case one is lost or needs temporary securing. Side repeaters are not required on race cars. If the windscreen is damaged on the driver's side, it can be rotated as a workaround before replacement."},{"title":"Driver And Session Reminders","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#driver-and-session-reminders","summary":"Keep a short list on the dash for session reminders. Use Rain-X on the windscreen and visor, inside and outside.","keywords":"Keep a short list on the dash for session reminders. Use Rain-X on the windscreen and visor, inside and outside."},{"title":"Brake And Fluid Notes","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#brake-and-fluid-notes","summary":"Fit the race master cylinder cap, 77175, and use a sweatband to catch seepage. Buy a brake bleed kit or similar. Clean up brake fluid with water.","keywords":"Fit the race master cylinder cap, 77175, and use a sweatband to catch seepage. Buy a brake bleed kit or similar. Clean up brake fluid with water."},{"title":"Workshop Notes","section":"Spanner checks","url":"spanner-checks.html#workshop-notes","summary":"Do not over-torque wheel nuts; over-torque can stretch studs and make future loosening more likely. The Driver Guide notes that copper grease on wheel studs can help with frequent wheel removal; keep any grease away…","keywords":"Do not over-torque wheel nuts; over-torque can stretch studs and make future loosening more likely. The Driver Guide notes that copper grease on wheel studs can help with frequent wheel removal; keep any grease away from braking surfaces. Release the torque wrench back to its storage setting at the end of the day."}];
